: external resources such as OMIM, Orphanet, or GARD may retire or obsolete a term. For example \"obsolete Ehlers-Danlos syndrome with periventricular heterotopia\" (MONDO:0019348).\ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:34:        - IAO:0000423: to be replaced with external ontology term\ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:66:1. Run the [risky_obsoletion_check](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/risky_obsoletion_check) pipeline to check if external ontologies and databases (such as EFO and ClinGen) are using this Mondo term.\ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:127:    1. If there is a rare disease subset annotation from the obsolete term,  it is ok to keep the rare subset annotation to the term kept (we will let the rare disease authorities know so that they can update the source of truth. \ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:155:1. remove all rare disease subset annotations\ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:169:1. Remove all rare disease subset annotations\ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:182:1. Remove all rare disease subset annotations\nsrc/patterns/data/ld-mondo/ld-mondo-disease_series_by_gene.tsv:1883:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0012238\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 2\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000090\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\thttp://identifiers.org/hgnc/10990\tSLC25A4\r\nsrc/patterns/data/ld-mondo/ld-mondo-disease_series_by_gene.tsv:1884:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0012241\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 3\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000090\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\thttp://identifiers.org/hgnc/1160\tTWNK\r\nsrc/patterns/data/ld-mondo/ld-mondo-disease_series_by_gene.tsv:1885:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0012415\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 4\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000090\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\thttp://identifiers.org/hgnc/9180\tPOLG2\r\nsrc/patterns/data/ld-mondo/ld-mondo-disease_series_by_gene.tsv:1886:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0013117\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 5\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000090\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\thttp://identifiers.org/hgnc/17296\tRRM2B\r\nsrc/patterns/data/ld-mondo/ld-mondo-disease_series_by_gene.tsv:1887:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0009783\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 1\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0016810\tautosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia\thttp://identifiers.org/hgnc/9179\tPOLG\r\nsrc/patterns/data/ld-mondo/ld-mondo-disease_series_by_gene.tsv:1888:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0014656\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 2\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000090\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\thttp://identifiers.org/hgnc/18466\tRNASEH1\r\nsrc/patterns/data/ld-mondo/ld-mondo-disease_series_by_gene.tsv:1889:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0014898\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 3; PEOB3\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0016810\tautosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia\thttp://identifiers.org/hgnc/11831\tTK2\r\nsrc/patterns/data/ld-mondo/ld-mondo-disease_series_by_gene.tsv:1890:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0024528\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial dna deletions, autosomal dominant 1\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0008003\tautosomal dominant progressive external ophthalmoplegia\thttp://identifiers.org/hgnc/9179\tPOLG\r\ndocs/editors-guide/synching.md:6:1. **Mapping** creates links between existing Mondo classes and external ids\ndocs/editors-guide/synching.md:16:1. We may get disease to gene associations from external groups as well, such as ClinGen, who often request new gene-related disease terms.\ndocs/editors-guide/project-management.md:18:**Technical Team** | Implement technical workflows including mappings and external source ingests, ontology statistics, and quality control checks. | [Nico Matentzoglu](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7356-1779), [Trish Whetzel](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3458-4839), [Joe Flack](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2906-7319) | [tech](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/issues?q=is%3Aopen+is%3Aissue+label%3Atech), [qc-call](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/issues?q=is%3Aopen+is%3Aissue+label%3Aqc-call), [QC](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/issues?q=is%3Aopen+is%3Aissue+label%3Atech+label%3AQC)\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:17:# Tests if an animal disease made it into the rare disease subset\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:936:###  qc-preferred-external-no-equivalent.sparql\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:1013:#description: No two Mondo IDs should ever point to the same external ID\nsrc/patterns/data/ld-mondo/ld-mondo-autosomal_recessive.tsv:39:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0016810\tautosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0005181\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia\r\ndocs/editors-guide/exclusion-reasons.md:21:_This document is a work in progress._ Noted below are reasons for excluding external ontology or terminology terms from Mondo. See enum file [here](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/blob/master/src/schema/mondo.yaml). The dashes in column 1 are meant to indicate the code is a subtype of the code above.\ndocs/editors-guide/exclusion-reasons.md:33:| --- MONDO:excludeExternalFactor             | external factor                                          | Code used when the term is excluded from Mondo based on the fact that the term's concept is not a disease and represents factors and/or actions that influence health status and might (or might not) be the cause of a disease.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                | This code should be used when the concept is not clearly a procedure, an event, or a medical action, or is a mix of these.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  | ICD10CM:Z00-Z99\t- Factors influencing health status and contact with health services                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |                                     TRUE                                    |\ndocs/editors-guide/exclusion-reasons.md:39:| --- MONDO:excludeComplication               | complication                                             | Code used when the term is excluded from Mondo based on the fact that the term's concept is not a disease and represents a complication to an external procedure (e.g surgery) or events (e.g birth).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           | One needs to be careful to not confuse actual \"diseases\" resulting from an event (e.g. injury, disease resulting from infection, or viral sequela). In these cases, the representation of the concept in Mondo might be reasonable.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | ICD10CM:E36-E36 - Intraoperative complications of endocrine system Orphanet:90053 - Complications after hematopoietic stem cell transplantation                                                                                                                                                                              |                                     TRUE                                    |\ndocs/editors-guide/update-external-sources.md:1:# Update external sources\nsrc/patterns/data/matches/carcinoma.tsv:44:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0002944\texternal ear carcinoma\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001691\texternal ear\r\ndocs/editors-guide/related-documentation.md:14:| Lexmatch pipeline (MISSING) | A description of the lexical match pipeline that is used to find matches between Mondo terms and external ontologies. | Mondo curators and developers |\ndocs/editors-guide/import-terms-for-logical-axioms.md:5:This workflow is for adding classes from external ontologies (e.g. GO, CHEBI, HGNC, or NCBI) and is much more streamlined compared to [MIREOTing](https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/wiki/Editor-Guide#mireoting).\ndocs/editors-guide/import-terms-for-logical-axioms.md:7:As a Mondo curator, when you have a ticket that requires a term from an external ontology, create a new git feature branch to include _only_ the changes for the refresh of the imports following the steps below. Also, ask on Slack if other curators need other terms imported. \ndocs/editors-guide/import-terms-for-logical-axioms.md:42:1. Once the PR is approved and merged, the terms imported from external ontologies can be referenced in logical definitions.\ndocs/editors-guide/import-terms-for-logical-axioms.md:70:While there are alternate approaches to add classes from external ontologies, the instructions above are the only process that should be followed for importing external ontology classes into Mondo.\ndocs/editors-guide/proxy-merge.md:3:A proxy merge is caused by two equivalentTo mappings two the same external ID:\ndocs/editors-guide/proxy-merge.md:10:However, cases like this, informally referred to as reverse proxy merges, are allowed: two external concepts a considered the same by Mondo, for example:\nsrc/patterns/data/matches/location_top.tsv:65:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0002776\texternal ear disorder\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001691\texternal ear\r\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control.md:8:1. Proxy merge checking to ensure no external class is referenced by more than one MONDO class\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control.md:37:# if this fails, the resolution is to look for two MONDO classes with equivalence to the same external class\nsrc/patterns/data/matches/neoplasm.tsv:57:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0021235\texternal ear neoplasm\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001691\texternal ear\r\ndocs/editors-guide/mondo-ingest-updates.md:3:## Bulk update deprecated external classes\ndocs/editors-guide/mondo-ingest-updates.md:5:In order to accurately reflect an external concept is obsoleted, we have a \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:3:Rare diseases in Mondo are created based on 3 main subsets. Rules for editing these subsets depends on the subset type. Before editing rare disease information in Mondo, please familiarize yourself with how rare diseases are represented in Mondo [here](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset/).\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:5:It is important to remember that the rare disease subsets are updated automatically at the time of the monthly Mondo release. It is, therefore, crucial that editing rare disease information is done by following the instructions below for these edits to be retained after the release.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:8:These terms have an `in_subset` annotation `X_rare`  (where X is the rare disease authority).\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:10:- a list of rare diseases is maintained for each source here:\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:15:Until we have set a process with the sources to maintain this list automatically, Mondo editors should update this list of rare diseases manually **upon request by the authoritative source**.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:16:1. Create or open a GitHub issue reported by the authoritative source (ie NORD, GARD) to add or remove a term from the authoritative rare disease list.   \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:17:1. Open the rare disease list (from src/ontology/subsets) in a text editor:\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:20:1. The term will be added to the rare disease list once the PR is merged, but the subset annotation will not be added until the monthly Mondo release has run.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:22:Note: rare diseases from Orphanet are maintained by a completely automated process. If an update is needed, please create a GitHub issue for the technical team.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:24:**2. Manually curated rare diseases.**\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:25:These terms have an `in_subset` annotation `mondo_curated_rare`, and should be added manually when they are reported in the literature as 'rare', but they are not reported in the rare disease authorities' list.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:34:3. Share the GH issue reporting this term as a rare disease with the rare disease authoritative sources (by bringing their attention to this term, they might want to add it to their rare disease list).\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:36:**3. Inferred rare disease.**\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:37:These terms have an `in_subset` annotation `inferred_rare`. This subset is created automatically and refers to the ontological children of rare disease terms.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:41:#### Note about rare disease subset during term obsoletion/merging.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:42:Specific rules exist surrounding obsoletion and merging of terms that are in the rare disease subset (see [here](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset/) and below).\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:43:Since rare disease subsets (except for the mondo_curated_rare subset) are maintained automatically or semi-automatically, obsoleting/merging terms from the rare disease subset should be done with extreme caution.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:47:**Obsoletion/merging of rare disease terms: rules.**\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:48:1. If a MONDO term (MONDO id) is obsoleted, the Mondo term will not appear in the rare disease subset, regardless of whether the disease is considered rare by one of the authoritative rare disease sources.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:49:2. If an authoritative rare source obsoletes a disease term or removes a disease from their rare disease list, the corresponding Mondo term will lose its corresponding \"X_rare\" subset annotation for this particular source.\nsrc/patterns/data/matches/disease_series_by_gene_and_inheritance.tsv:16:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0009783\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 1\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0016810\tautosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia\thttp://identifiers.org/hgnc/9179\tPOLG\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0000007\tAutosomal recessive inheritance\r\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:3:A rare disease is a disease that affects fewer than 200,000 people in the United States. However, this definition is not universal and varies between rare disease sources, geographical locations, etc. Consequently, rare disease criteria also vary between sources, and one might have difficulties reconciling sources and creating a list for rare diseases. Mondo recognizes these impediments and differences of view. This document explains how Mondo handles rare diseases, how one could get a rare disease list, and the efforts made to bring the community together.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:5:#### What are rare diseases in Mondo?\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:6:There are 3 sources for rare diseases in Mondo:\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:7:1. rare disease authorities\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:11:**1. rare disease authorities**\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:12:We rely on rare disease authorities (see table below) to determine diseases recognized as “rare”. These rare diseases are determined by the rare disease authorities according to their own criteria. When a disease is considered “rare” according to a source, the corresponding Mondo term is annotated with an `in_subset` annotation expressing that this disease is considered rare according to this source: `X_rare`  (where X is the rare disease authority). These rare disease subset annotations are maintained automatically via rare disease lists from the authorities.  \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:19:In some occasional cases, some diseases are reported in the literature as 'rare', though they are not reported in the rare disease authorities' list. In these cases, we record the disease as rare by manually adding the \"mondo_curated_rare\" subset annotation to the term. These manually curated annotations must be supported by the literature. This new rare disease is reported to the rare disease authorities. \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:22:Ontological parents pass their characteristics (here: axioms) to their children. We leverage this ontological property for rare diseases: ontological children of a term that is considered \"rare\" according to the rules above are getting the \"inferred_rare\" subset annotation.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:25:#### The Mondo rare disease subset includes ALL rare diseases according to ALL rare disease sources.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:26:The Mondo rare disease subset represents the UNION of rare diseases according to all authority sources, manual curation, and ontology inference. It should be noted that most of the diseases in this subset will be viewed as “rare” by _most_ authorities, but some will be viewed as “rare” by _only some_ authorities. \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:30:- terms with the annotation in_subset ‘X_rare’ (which includes ALL the rare disease authorities represented in Mondo)\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:31:- terms with the annotation in_subset ‘mondo_curated_rare’ (representing the manually curated rare diseases)\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:34:Users should decide which subset they want to use for their applications. They can do so by filtering the rare disease subset by any of the 'in_subset' annotations. \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:36:#### Where to find the Mondo rare disease subset, and what is included in the file?\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:37:The Mondo rare disease can be found as a product of the Mondo release, available in .obo and .json formats\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:41:It should be noted that these rare disease subset files include, in addition to the \"rare diseases\" (as defined above), the ontological parents (ie subclasses) of these rare diseases. These ontological parents allow to view the rare disease subset in a higher classification. \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:43:To get **only** the rare diseases, one should filter the subset files for terms annotated with \"subset:rare\" (or the subset annotation refering to the rare disease authoritative source of choice).\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:45:#### What happens when a rare disease term is obsoleted or loses its \"rare\" characteristic?\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:46:Terms in Mondo can be obsoleted for multiple reasons (see obsoletion documents [here](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting/)). It is possible that some of these terms to be obsoleted are in the rare disease subset. \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:48:1. If a MONDO term (MONDO id) is obsoleted, the Mondo term will not appear in the rare disease subset, regardless of whether the disease is considered rare by one of the authoritative rare disease sources. The rare disease authoritative source reporting this disease will be contacted to report this obsoletion and its reasons.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:49:2. If an authoritative rare source obsoletes a disease term or removes a disease from their rare disease list, the corresponding Mondo term will lose its corresponding \"rare subset\" annotation for this particular source. It should be noted that the rare subset annotation for the other authorities will not be modified unless they also obsolete or remove the corresponding disease from their rare disease list.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:52:#### Current rare disease authority sources represented in Mondo\ndocs/editors-guide/risky_obsoletion_check.md:5:The pipeline outlined below will check if external databases or ontologies (such as ClinGen or EFO) are using our Mondo terms in their projects. If so, we should reconsider if we want to obsolete these terms. If we do, we need to notify them that the terms are candidates for obsoletion.\ndocs/editors-guide/risky_obsoletion_check.md:9:1. This will output a list of terms that are in use by an external user. Note, you will see Error 1 at the end if there are terms being used by external sources.\ndocs/editors-guide/risky_obsoletion_check.md:11:1. If we want to continue with obsoleting terms that are used by external sources, add obsoletion tags to the terms via the [ROBOT template](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1tt1Wk70j9XiHLV1vKQyNiHhaazh286pobpJk1ecSCCg/edit#gid=505727337). (See instructions [here](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/robot-template/) to merge the ROBOT template.\nsrc/patterns/data/matches/location.tsv:180:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0002776\texternal ear disorder\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000001\tdisease or disorder\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001691\texternal ear\r\nsrc/patterns/data/matches/location.tsv:181:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000973\texternal ear lipoma\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0005106\tlipoma\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001691\texternal ear\r\nsrc/patterns/data/matches/location.tsv:345:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000262\totomycosis\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0002041\tfungal infectious disease\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001691\texternal ear\r\nsrc/patterns/data/matches/autosomal_recessive.tsv:19:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0016810\tautosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0005181\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia\r\nsrc/patterns/data/matches/autosomal_dominant.tsv:23:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0008003\tautosomal dominant progressive external ophthalmoplegia\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0005181\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia\r\nsrc/patterns/data/matches/lipoma.tsv:11:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000973\texternal ear lipoma\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001691\texternal ear\r\nsrc/patterns/data/matches/squamous_cell_carcinoma.tsv:16:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0003501\texternal ear squamous cell carcinoma\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001691\texternal ear\r\nsrc/patterns/data/matches/cancer.tsv:61:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0003574\texternal ear cancer\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001691\texternal ear\r\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:2:A subset of the extensive Mondo QC checks are automatically run on the externally managed content (EMC). To limit delay due to QC failure, any content from external providers that does not pass these QC checks is not included in Mondo until the content is updated by the data providers and passes the QC checks. Reports of these QC checks will be available for review of any errors so that any needed changes can be made.\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:7:| duplicate_scoped_synonym | The synonym from the external source is the same as a synonym already reported on the Mondo term, but the synonym scope is not the same. For example, the synonym from the source is \"exact\", and the synonym in Mondo is \"broad\".| <a href='https://robot.obolibrary.org/report_queries/duplicate_scoped_synonym' target='_blank'>duplicate scoped synonym</a> |\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:12:| qc-animal-disease-rare| An animal disease is contained within a rare disease subset. |<a href='https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/blob/master/src/sparql/qc/mondo/qc-animal-disease-rare.sparql' target='_blank'>animal disease rare</a>|\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:13:| qc-proxy-merges| A mapping in the EMC file caused two different Mondo classes to have a mapping to the same external source term. See <a href='https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/proxy-merge/' target='_blank'>proxy merges</a> for more information.| <a href='https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/blob/master/src/sparql/qc/mondo/qc-proxy-merges.sparql' target='_blank'>proxy merge</a>|\nsrc/patterns/data/matches_annotations/autosomal_recessive.tsv:6:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0016810\tautosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0005181\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia\thttp://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label\r\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:17:    * TODO: still to clean up a lot of synonym scopes seeded from external ontologies\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:73:These are incorrect in many places where they have been brought in externally. **Do not trust scope if there is no synonym xref other than DO**.\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:112:dubious synonym | DUBIOUS | Not to be relied upon or suspect | MONDO:0002776 'external ear disease'\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:113:Synonym to be removed from public release but maintained in edit version as record of external usage | EXCLUDE | Some synonyms are annotated with EXCLUDE, e.g. “NOS” (not otherwise specified) synonyms. It is useful to have these in the edit version, but these are filtered on release. | MONDO:0011088 congenital myasthenic syndrome 1A, MONDO:0002679 cerebral infarction, MONDO:0008170 'ovarian cancer'\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:126:The seed version of Mondo was created from external ontologies. We preserve both original axiom annotations where they are provided (e.g. DO) and we also stamp the originating ID.\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:208:MONDO:otherHierarchy | The external term Mondo is referencing is not in a \"disease\" branch. | xrefs | N | The NCIT:C123248 xref on MONDO:0007080\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:213:MONDO:typo | An xref from an external source is a typo. | xrefs | N | MONDO:0019572 autosomal recessive cutis laxa type 1\nsrc/patterns/data/matches_annotations/autosomal_dominant.tsv:9:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0008003\tautosomal dominant progressive external ophthalmoplegia\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0005181\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia\thttp://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label\r\nsrc/patterns/data/matches_annotations/lipoma.tsv:3:http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000973\texternal ear lipoma\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001691\texternal ear\thttp://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label\r\ndocs/editors-guide/curation-workflow-overview.md:11:  2. 'Slurping' from [external sources](https://mondo.monarchinitiative.org/pages/sources/) - this is our pipeline to ingest new terms from external sources (such as OMIM, Orphanet), to keep Mondo synched with the external sources.\ndocs/editors-guide/strategic-refinement.md:31:1. the tab [candidates.tsv](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1e8S9sjE2kmnCOvmqI98CQ7HJoSPh8HyV9CEP4ysI07I/edit#gid=2039272934) has grouping classes, that have xrefs to ORDO and do not have any xrefs from any other external source \ndocs/editors-guide/mappings.md:7:1. Cross-references are imported from existing sources (other ontologies, external sources, see metadata on the cross references).\ndocs/editors-guide/mappings.md:16:- broad (external term is broader in scope)  \ndocs/editors-guide/mappings.md:17:- narrow (external term is narrower in scope)  \ndocs/editors-guide/mappings.md:18:- related (external term is in a different branch, i.e. a disease in Mondo to a phenotype in HPO)  \ndocs/analysis.md:3:**_The information on this page reflects past practices and does not represent our current implementation. We are currently relying on \"rare subset\" to identify rare diseases in Mondo. Please refer to [this documentation page](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset/) or contact our team directly for more information._**\ndocs/analysis.md:6:To answer the question of 'How many rare diseases are there?' we analyzed terms in Mondo to get a total count of Rare Diseases as defined in Mondo Disease Ontology (Mondo).\ndocs/analysis.md:25:This gives us a list of `21563` Mondo rare disease terms.\ndocs/analysis.md:32:There are `12914` Mondo rare disease terms.\ndocs/analysis.md:39:We add these terms to our set of Mondo rare disease terms.\ndocs/analysis.md:41:This increases the Mondo rare disease term count to `13866`.\ndocs/analysis.md:46:After considering only leaf nodes, we get `10394` as the final count of Mondo rare disease terms.\ndocs/analysis.md:51:[all-mondo-disease-terms.tsv](artifacts/all-mondo-disease-terms.tsv): As part of our analysis, we generated a TSV containing 21633 Mondo disease terms, each with annotations that signifies whether the term is a rare disease term and whether that term is a leaf node in the ontology.\nsrc/ontology/mappings/mondo_exactmatch_omimps.sssom.tsv:27:MONDO:0000090\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\tskos:exactMatch\tOMIMPS:157640\tProgressive external ophthalmoplegia with mtDNA deletions\tsemapv:ManualMappingCuration\nsrc/patterns/README.md:14:## Reverse engineering from external ontologies based on lexical patterns\nsrc/ontology/mappings/mondo_exactmatch_orphanet.sssom.tsv:210:MONDO:0005181\tprogressive external ophthalmoplegia\tskos:exactMatch\tOrphanet:520820\tProgressive external ophthalmoplegia\tsemapv:ManualMappingCuration\nsrc/ontology/mappings/mondo_exactmatch_orphanet.sssom.tsv:621:MONDO:0007587\texternal auditory canal atresia-vertical talus-hypertelorism syndrome\tskos:exactMatch\t