rc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:45162:name: external ear squamous cell carcinoma\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:45163:def: \"A squamous cell carcinoma that arises from the skin of the external ear.\" [NCIT:C6083]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:45166:synonym: \"epidermoid carcinoma of external Ear\" EXACT [NCIT:C6083]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:45167:synonym: \"epidermoid carcinoma of the external Ear\" EXACT [DOID:5538, NCIT:C6083]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:45168:synonym: \"epidermoid carcinoma of the external ear\" EXACT [DOID:5538, NCIT:C6083]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:45169:synonym: \"external Ear epidermoid carcinoma\" EXACT [NCIT:C6083]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:45170:synonym: \"external Ear squamous cell carcinoma\" EXACT [DOID:5538, NCIT:C6083]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:45171:synonym: \"external ear squamous cell carcinoma\" EXACT [DOID:5538, MONDO:patterns/location, NCIT:C6083]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:45172:synonym: \"squamous cell carcinoma of external Ear\" EXACT [NCIT:C6083]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:45173:synonym: \"squamous cell carcinoma of the external Ear\" EXACT [NCIT:C6083]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:45179:is_a: MONDO:0002944 ! external ear carcinoma\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:45181:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/external_ear_squamous_cell_carcinoma\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46324:name: external ear cancer\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46325:def: \"A malignant neoplasm involving the external ear.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6601-2165]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46326:synonym: \"cancer of external ear\" EXACT [MONDO:patterns/cancer]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46327:synonym: \"external ear cancer\" EXACT [DOID:5665, MONDO:patterns/location]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46328:synonym: \"malignant external Ear neoplasm\" EXACT [NCIT:C4653]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46329:synonym: \"malignant external ear neoplasm\" EXACT [MONDO:patterns/cancer, NCIT:C4653]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46330:synonym: \"malignant external Ear tumor\" EXACT [NCIT:C4653]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46331:synonym: \"malignant external Ear tumour\" EXACT OMO:0003005 []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46332:synonym: \"malignant neoplasm of external Ear\" EXACT [NCIT:C4653]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46333:synonym: \"malignant neoplasm of external ear\" EXACT [MONDO:patterns/cancer, NCIT:C4653]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46334:synonym: \"malignant neoplasm of the external Ear\" EXACT [DOID:5665, NCIT:C4653]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46335:synonym: \"malignant neoplasm of the external ear\" EXACT [DOID:5665, NCIT:C4653]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46336:synonym: \"malignant tumor of external Ear\" EXACT [DOID:5665, NCIT:C4653]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46337:synonym: \"malignant tumor of the external Ear\" EXACT [NCIT:C4653]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46338:synonym: \"malignant tumour of external Ear\" EXACT OMO:0003005 []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46339:synonym: \"malignant tumour of the external Ear\" EXACT OMO:0003005 []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:46347:is_a: MONDO:0021235 ! external ear neoplasm\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:51972:def: \"An infiltrating adenocarcinoma derived from ceruminous glands in the external auditory canal.\" [NCIT:P378]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:51984:is_a: MONDO:0002944 ! external ear carcinoma\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:58342:def: \"Diseases of the ninth cranial (glossopharyngeal) nerve or its nuclei in the medulla. The nerve may be injured by diseases affecting the lower brain stem, floor of the posterior fossa, jugular foramen, or the nerve's extracranial course. Clinical manifestations include loss of sensation from the pharynx, decreased salivation, and syncope. Glossopharyngeal neuralgia refers to a condition that features recurrent unilateral sharp pain in the tongue, angle of the jaw, external auditory meatus and throat that may be associated with syncope. Episodes may be triggered by cough, sneeze, swallowing, or pressure on the tragus of the ear. (Adams et al., Principles of Neurology, 6th ed, p1390)\" [MESH:D020435]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:74393:name: progressive external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:74401:synonym: \"chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:74402:synonym: \"chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia [ambiguous]\" EXACT []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:74403:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia\" EXACT [DOID:12558, ICD10CM:H49.4, icd11.foundation:1698427219, ICD9CM:378.72, Orphanet:520820]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:74421:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/chronic_progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:81958:def: \"A sexually transmitted papillary growth caused by the human papillomavirus. It usually arises in the skin and mucous membranes of the perianal region and external genitalia.\" [NCIT:P378]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:102495:def: \"Overproduction of parathyroid hormone in response to influence external to the parathyroid glands.\" [NCIT:P378]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:102526:def: \"A condition with a clinical picture similar to that of Parkinson disease, but which is caused by external factors, including medication.\" [NCIT:P378]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:106758:def: \"Distal arthrogryposis type 5 is an inherited developmental defect syndrome characterized by multiple congenital contractures of limbs, without primary neurologic and/or muscle disease that affects limb function, and ocular anomalies (ptosis, external ophtalmoplegia and/or strabismus). Intelligence is normal.\" [Orphanet:1154]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:107115:name: atresia of external auditory canal and conductive deafness\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:107119:synonym: \"atresia of external auditory canal and conductive deafness\" EXACT [OMIM:108760]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:107125:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/atresia_of_external_auditory_canal_and_conductive_deafness\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:107407:def: \"Spinocerebellar ataxia type 3 (SCA3), also known as Machado-Joseph disease, is the most common subtype of type 1 autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia (ADCA type 1), a neurodegenerative disorder, and is characterized by ataxia, external progressive ophthalmoplegia, and other neurological manifestations.\" [Orphanet:98757]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:111465:def: \"Autosomal dominant popliteal pterygium syndrome (AD-PPS) is a rare genetic malformative disorder characterized by cleft lip, with or without cleft palate, contractures of the lower extremities, abnormal external genitalia, syndactyly of fingers and/or toes, and a pyramidal skin fold over the hallux nail.\" [Orphanet:1300]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:113780:def: \"Gorham-Stout disease (GSD) is a rare disease of massive osteolysis associated with proliferation and dilation of lymphatic vessels. GSD may affect any bone in the body and can be monostotic or polyostotic. Symptoms at presentation are dependent upon the location(s) of the disease; the most common symptom is localized pain. The disease may be discovered after a pathological fracture.\" [Orphanet:73]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:113990:def: \"Deafness-ear malformation-facial palsy syndrome is characterized by profound conductive deafness due to stapedial abnormalities associated with variable malformations of the external ears and facial paralysis. It has been described in three sibs and their mother. Inheritance is autosomal dominant.\" [Orphanet:3232]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:117594:name: external auditory canal atresia-vertical talus-hypertelorism syndrome\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:117601:synonym: \"external auditory canal, bilateral atresia of, with congenital vertical talus\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:117602:synonym: \"inherited congenital bilateral atresia of the external auditory canal, congenital bilateral vertical talus and increased interocular distance\" RELATED [GARD:0004638]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:117614:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/external_auditory_canal_atresia_vertical_talus_hypertelorism_syndrome\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:117615:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/external_auditory_canal_bilateral_atresia_of_with_congenital_vertical_talus\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:120241:def: \"Oculoauriculovertebral spectrum (OAVS) with radial defects is a rare branchial arches and limb primordia development disorder characterized by variable degrees of uni- or bilateral craniofacial malformation and radial defects that result in extremely variable phenotypic manifestations. Characteristic features include low postnatal weight, short stature, vertebral defects, hearing loss, and facial dysmorphism (incl. facial asymmetry, external, middle, and inner ear malformations, orofacial clefts, and mandibular hypoplasia). These features are invariably associated with radial defects, such as preaxial polydactyly, thumb and/or radius hypoplasia/agenesis, or triphalangeal thumb. Cardiac, pulmonary, renal, and central nervous system involvement has also been reported.\" [Orphanet:2549]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:122710:synonym: \"radial Ray defects, hearing impairment, external ophthalmoplegia, and thrombocytopenia\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:122711:synonym: \"radial ray defects, hearing impairment, external ophthalmoplegia, and thrombocytopenia\" EXACT [DOID:0111381, OMIM:147750, Orphanet:2307]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:122737:def: \"Johnson neuroectodermal syndrome is characterized by alopecia, anosmia or hyposmia, conductive deafness with malformed ears and microtia and/or atresia of the external auditory canal, and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism.\" [Orphanet:2316]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:125647:def: \"Nager syndrome, also called Nager acrofacial dysostosis (NAFD) is a congenital malformation syndrome characterized by mandibulofacial dystosis (malar hypoplasia, micrognathia, external ear malformations) and variable preaxial limb defects.\" [Orphanet:245]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:127136:name: autosomal dominant progressive external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:127137:def: \"Autosomal dominant form of progressive external ophthalmoplegia.\" [MONDO:patterns/autosomal_dominant]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:127146:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 1\" RELATED [MONDO:Lexical]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:127147:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant type 1\" EXACT [MONDO:RULE_1]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:127148:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia, autosomal dominant\" EXACT [MONDO:patterns/autosomal_dominant]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:127154:is_a: MONDO:0000090 ! progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:127156:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia_with_mitochondrial_dna_deletions_autosomal_dominant_1\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:127534:def: \"A condition that affects the skin, hair, mucosa (areas ofthe body that are lined with mucus), gums (gingiva), eyes, nose and lungs. Symptoms typically begin in infancy and may include development of cataracts (clouding of the eye lens); blindness; hair loss (alopecia); abnormal changes to the perineum (the area between the anus and external genitalia); and small, skin-colored bumps (keratosis pilaris). Terminal lung disease has also been reported. The cause of HMD is thought to be an abnormality in desmosomes and gap junctions, which are structures involved in cell-to-cell contact. HMD typically follows autosomal dominant inheritance, but has occurred sporadically (in an individual who has no family history of the condition). Treatment typically focuses on individual symptoms of the condition.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5208-3432, https://rarediseases.info.nih.gov/diseases/5427/hereditary-mucoepithelial-dysplasia]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:135989:def: \"Radio-renal syndrome is a rare developmental defect during embryogenesis characterized by variable upper limb reduction defects and renal anomalies. Patients typically present absence/hypoplasia of digits, radii and/or ulnae, short stature and mild external ear malformation, as well as kidney agenesis or ectopia. There have been no further descriptions in the literature since 1983.\" [Orphanet:3015]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:136585:def: \"Autosomal dominant Robinow syndrome (DRS) is the more common type of Robinow syndrome (RS) characterized by mild to moderate limb shortening and abnormalities of the head, face and external genitalia.\" [Orphanet:3107]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:143619:synonym: \"microcephaly, IUGR, hypertelorism, ptosis, iris coloboma, hooked nose, external ear dysplasia, psychomotor retardation\" RELATED [GARD:0007896]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:143902:def: \"Ablepharon macrostomia syndrome is an extremely rare multiple congenital malformation syndrome characterized by the association of ablepharon, macrostomia, abnormal external ears, syndactyly of the hands and feet, skin findings (such as dry and coarse skin or redundant folds of skin), absent or sparse hair, genital malformations and developmental delay (in 2/3 of cases). Other reported manifestations include malar hypoplasia, absent or hypoplastic nipples, umbilical abnormalities and growth retardation. It is a mainly sporadic disorder, although a few familial cases having been reported, and it displays significant clinical overlap with Fraser syndrome.\" [Orphanet:920]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:144576:def: \"Acrocraniofacial dysostosis is a very rare form of acrofacial dyosotosis, reported in two sisters to date, characterized by short stature, acrocephaly, ocular hypertelorism, ptosis of eyelids, ocular proptosis, downslanting palpebral fissures, high nasal bridge, anteverted nostrils, short philtrum, cleft palate, micrognathia, abnormal external ears, preauricular pits, mixed hearing loss, bulbous digits, metatarsus varus, pectus excavatum and various radiological abnormalities. Features of this syndrome were reported to overlap with otopalatodigital syndrome types 1 and 2. There have been no further descriptions in the literature since 1988.\" [Orphanet:949]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:145060:def: \"A type of arthrogryposis characterized by congenital cleft palate, microcephaly, craniostenosis and arthrogryposis (limitation of extension of elbows, flexed adducted thumbs, camptodactyly and clubfeet). Additional features include facial dysmorphism (\\\"myopathic\\\" stiff face, antimongoloid slanting, external ophthalmoplegia, telecanthus, low-set large malrotated ears, open mouth, mierogenia and high arched palate). Velopharyngeal insufficiency with difficulties in swallowing, increased secretion of the nose and throat, prominent occiput, generalized muscular hypotonia with mild cyanosis and no spontaneous movements, seizures, torticollis, areflexia, intellectual disability, hypertrichosis of the lower extremities, and scleredema (in the first days of life) are also observed. The disease often leads to early death. Transmission is autosomal recessive. No new cases of adducted thumbs-arthrogryposis, Christian type have been described since 1983.\" [Orphanet:2952]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:151226:def: \"Cereballar ataxia - ectodermal dysplasia is a very rare disease, characterized by hypodontia and sparse hair in combination with cerebellar ataxia and normal intelligence. Imaging demonstrates a cerebellar atrophy.\" [Orphanet:1174]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:152781:def: \"Verloove Vanhorick-Brubakk syndrome is a multiple congenital anomalies/dysmorphic syndrome characterized by multiple skeletal malformations (short femora and humeri, bilateral absence of metatarsal and metacarpal bone in hands and feet, bilateral partial syndactyly of fingers and toes or oligopolysyndactyly, deformed lumbosacral spine), congenital heart disease (truncus arteriosus), lung and urogenital malformations (bilateral bilobar lungs, horseshoe kidney, cryptorchidism), and facial malformations (bilateral cleft lip and palate, micrognathia, small, low-set ears without external meatus). It is lethal in the neonatal period. There have been no further descriptions in the literature since 1981.\" [Orphanet:3429]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:155388:name: conductive deafness-malformed external ear syndrome\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:155395:synonym: \"conductive deafness - malformed external ear\" RELATED [GARD:0001460]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:155396:synonym: \"conductive deafness with malformed external ear\" RELATED [GARD:0001460]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:155398:synonym: \"conductive hearing loss-malformed external ear syndrome\" EXACT [Orphanet:3216]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:155399:synonym: \"deafness, conductive, with malformed external EAR\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:155410:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/deafness_conductive_with_malformed_external_ear\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:155420:def: \"Conductive deafness-ptosis-skeletal anomalies syndrome is a rare, genetic ectodermal dysplasia syndrome characterized by conductive hearing loss due to atresia of the external auditory canal and the middle ear complicated by chronic infection, ptosis and skeletal anomalies (internal rotation of hips, dislocation of the radial heads and fifth finger clinodactyly). In addition, a thin, pinched nose, delayed hair growth and dysplastic teeth are associated. There have been no further descriptions in the literature since 1978.\" [Orphanet:3236]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:174402:name: congenital multicore myopathy with external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:174411:synonym: \"minicore myopathy with external ophthalmoplegia\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:174414:synonym: \"multicore myopathy with external ophthalmoplegia\" RELATED [GARD:0010316]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:174415:synonym: \"multiminicore disease with external ophthalmoplegia\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:174416:synonym: \"multiminicore myopathy multicore myopathy with external ophthalmoplegia\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:174426:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/congenital_multicore_myopathy_with_external_ophthalmoplegia\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176677:name: progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 1\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176678:def: \"Any autosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia in which the cause of the disease is a mutation in the POLG gene.\" [MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176682:synonym: \"autosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia\" RELATED [GARD:0001191]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176683:synonym: \"autosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia caused by mutation in POLG\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176684:synonym: \"cerebellar ataxia infantile with progressive external ophthalmoplegia\" RELATED [GARD:0001191]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176686:synonym: \"POLG autosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern, MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176687:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with cerebellar ataxia infantile\" RELATED [GARD:0001191]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176688:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 1\" EXACT [MONDO:Lexical, OMIM:258450]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176689:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive type 1\" EXACT [MONDO:RULE_1]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176690:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia, autosomal recessive 1\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176696:is_a: MONDO:0000090 ! progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176697:is_a: MONDO:0016810 ! autosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176698:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia_with_mitochondrial_dna_deletions_autosomal_recessive_1\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:176699:property_value: seeAlso \"https://rarediseases.info.nih.gov/diseases/1191/progressive-external-ophthalmoplegia-autosomal-recessive-1\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"GARD:0001191\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:177111:def: \"A rare, genetic intellectual disability syndrome characterized by macrocephaly, hypotonia, dysmorphic facial features (wide forehead, ptosis, downslanting palpebral fissures, enlarged and calcified external ears, large jaw), sparse body hair, tall stature, and intellectual disability. Hearing loss, insulin-resistant diabetes, and progressive distal muscle wasting (leading to joint contractures) have also been reported in adulthood. Rare manifestations include behavioral abnormalities (aggression and restlessness), hypothyroidism, cerebral calcification, ataxia, and peripheral neuropathy.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5208-3432, Orphanet:3042]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:182107:def: \"Ulbright-Hodes syndrome is characterized by renal dysplasia, growth retardation, phocomelia or mesomelia, radiohumeral fusion, rib abnormalities, anomalies of the external genitalia and a potter-like facies. The syndrome has been described in three infants (one pair of sibs and an unrelated case), all of whom died shortly after birth from respiratory distress resulting from pulmonary hypoplasia and oligohydramnios caused by renal dysplasia. The mode of transmission appears to be autosomal recessive.\" [Orphanet:3404]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:183025:def: \"Autosomal recessive Robinow syndrome (RRS) is the less common type of Robinow syndrome (RS) characterized by short-limb dwarfism, costovertebral segmentation defects and abnormalities of the head, face and external genitalia.\" [Orphanet:1507]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:185068:def: \"Spondylocostal dysostosis-anal and genitourinary malformations syndrome is characterized by the association of spondylocostal dysostosis with anal and genitourinary malformations (anal atresia and agenesis of external and internal genitalia). To date, only four cases have been described in the literature. Autosomal recessive inheritance has been suggested.\" [Orphanet:94095]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:188247:synonym: \"familial visceral myopathy with external ophthalmoplegia\" RELATED [GARD:0005496]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:188248:synonym: \"intestinal pseudoobstruction with external ophthalmoplegia\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:188250:synonym: \"visceral myopathy - familial external ophthalmoplegia\" RELATED [GARD:0005496]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:188251:synonym: \"visceral myopathy, familial, with external ophthalmoplegia\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:188252:synonym: \"visceral myopathy-familial external ophthalmoplegia syndrome\" EXACT [Orphanet:1876]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:188262:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/visceral_myopathy_familial_with_external_ophthalmoplegia\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:204137:def: \"A rare neurodevelopmental disorder in the ciliopathy group that is lethal in males and characterized by variable anomalies including external malformations (craniofacial and digital), and possible involvement of the central nervous system (CNS) and of viscera (kidneys, pancreas and ovaries) in females.\" [Orphanet:2750]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:205058:synonym: \"progressive basal ganglion dysfunction, mental and growth retardation, external ophthalmoplegia, postnatal microcephaly and deafness\" RELATED [GARD:0009288]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:206024:def: \"46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis (46,XY CGD) is a disorder of sex development (DSD) associated with anomalies in gonadal development that result in the presence of female external and internal genitalia despite the 46,XY karyotype.\" [Orphanet:242]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:206468:def: \"Kearns-Sayre syndrome (KSS) is a mitochondrial disease characterized by progressive external ophthalmoplegia (PEO), pigmentary retinitis and an onset before the age of 20 years. Common additional features include deafness, cerebellar ataxia and heart block.\" [Orphanet:480]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:206477:synonym: \"chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia with myopathy\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:206490:synonym: \"ophthalmoplegia, progressive external, with ragged red fibers\" RELATED [GARD:0006817]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:206491:synonym: \"ophthalmoplegia, progressive external, with ragged red fibres\" RELATED OMO:0003005 []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:206492:synonym: \"ophthalmoplegia, progressive external, with ragged-Red fibers\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:206493:synonym: \"ophthalmoplegia, progressive external, with ragged-Red fibres\" RELATED OMO:0003005 []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:206511:is_a: MONDO:0005181 ! progressive external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:206713:def: \"Lethal infantile mitochondrial myopathy is a rare mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation disorder characterized by progressive generalized hypotonia, progressive external ophthalmoplegia and severe lactic acidosis, which results in early fatality (days to months after birth). Patients may present with lethargy and areflexia and may associate additional features, such as cardiomyopathy, renal dysfunction, liver involvement and seizures.\" [Orphanet:254857]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:207267:def: \"Chondrodysplasia - disorder of sex development is an extremely rare disorder of sex development, reported in only two siblings (one terminated in pregnancy) to date, characterized by the clinical features of 46,XY complete gonadal dysgenesis (normal external female genitalia, lack of pubertal development, primary amenorrhea, and hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism) in association with severe dwarfism with generalized chondrodysplasia (bell-shaped thorax, micromelia, brachydactyly). Other reported features in the live sibling included eye anomalies (hypoplastic irides, myopia, coloboma of optic disks), dysmorphic features (deep-set eyes, upslanting palpebral fissures, puffy eyelids, large ears and mouth, mild prognathism), muscular hypoplasia, mild intellectual deficiency and severe microcephaly with cerebellar vermis hypoplasia. An autosomal recessive inheritance has been suggested.\" [Orphanet:1422]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:209261:def: \"Waters-West syndrome is characterized by the association of lethal non-spherocytic, non-immune hemolytic anemia with abnormalities of the external genitalia (micropenis and hypospadias), flat occiput, dimpled earlobes, deep plantar creases, and increased space between the first and second toes. It has been described only once in two brothers who died a few hours after birth. The second-born infant had massive ascites and hepatosplenomegaly. The mother had two spontaneous abortions (at 6 and 12 weeks gestation) but gave birth to a normal girl, suggesting an autosomal or X-linked recessive mode of inheritance. Although the parents were not known to be consanguineous, they shared a French-Canadian and American Indian ethnic origin.\" [Orphanet:1046]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:209633:def: \"Loose anagen syndrome is a rare benign hair disorder affecting predominantly blond females in childhood and characterized by the presence of hair that can be easily and painlessly pulled out. Most of the hair is in the anagen phase and lacks an external epithelial sheath. Hair grows back quickly and the condition improves spontaneously with aging. Loose anagen hair can be associated with other anomalies, such as coloboma.\" [Orphanet:168]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:209967:def: \"A congenital malformation of the external ear, seen more frequently in males, that occurs sporadically or is inherited, that is characterized by unilateral (79-93% of cases, 60% of which involve the right ear) or bilateral small and abnormally shaped auricles and that is often associated with atresia or stenosis of the ear canal, attention deficit disorders and delayed language development. The variation in auricle size ranges from grade I, where the auricle is simply smaller than normal, to grade IV, also known as anotia, where there is a complete absence of the external ear and of the auditory canal.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5208-3432, Orphanet:83463]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:214087:def: \"A rare, congenital malformation syndrome characterized by the association of anterior ocular chamber cleavage disorder with developmental delay, short stature and congenital hypothyroidism. Additional manifestations include cerebellar hypoplasia, tracheal stenosis, narrow external auditory meatus, and hip dislocation. There have been no further description in the literature since 1995.\" [Orphanet:2321]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:220122:def: \"Limb-mammary syndrome (LMS) is a rare disease belonging to the group of ectodermal dysplasias.\" [Orphanet:69085]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:231633:synonym: \"ophthalmoplegia, progressive external, and scoliosis\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:231634:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia and scoliosis\" EXACT [Orphanet:2744]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:234875:synonym: \"anophthalmia clinical with micrognathia malformed ears digital anomalies and abnormal external genitalia\" RELATED [GARD:0003645]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:234876:synonym: \"anophthalmia, clinical, with micrognathia, malformed ears, digital anomalies, and abnormal external genitalia\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241853:name: progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 2\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241854:def: \"Any progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions in which the cause of the disease is a mutation in the SLC25A4 gene.\" [MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241859:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions caused by mutation in SLC25A4\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241860:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 2\" EXACT [MONDO:Lexical, OMIM:609283]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241861:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant type 2\" EXACT [MONDO:RULE_1]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241862:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia, autosomal dominant 2\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241863:synonym: \"SLC25A4 progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern, MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241870:is_a: MONDO:0008003 ! autosomal dominant progressive external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241871:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia_with_mitochondrial_dna_deletions_autosomal_dominant_2\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241942:name: progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 3\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241943:def: \"Any progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions in which the cause of the disease is a mutation in the TWNK gene.\" [MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241948:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions caused by mutation in TWNK\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241949:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 3\" EXACT [MONDO:Lexical, OMIM:609286]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241950:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant type 3\" EXACT [MONDO:RULE_1]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241951:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia, autosomal dominant 3\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241952:synonym: \"TWNK progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern, MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241959:is_a: MONDO:0008003 ! autosomal dominant progressive external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:241960:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia_with_mitochondrial_dna_deletions_autosomal_dominant_3\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:243631:synonym: \"external ophthalmoplegia with synergistic divergence\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:243632:synonym: \"external ophthalmoplegia, synergistic divergence, jaw winking, and oculocutaneous hypopigmentation\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:245904:name: progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 4\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:245905:def: \"Any progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions in which the cause of the disease is a mutation in the POLG2 gene.\" [MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:245909:synonym: \"POLG2 progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern, MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:245910:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions caused by mutation in POLG2\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:245911:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 4\" EXACT [MONDO:Lexical, OMIM:610131]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:245912:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant type 4\" EXACT [MONDO:RULE_1]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:245913:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia, autosomal dominant 4\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:245920:is_a: MONDO:0008003 ! autosomal dominant progressive external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:245921:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia_with_mitochondrial_dna_deletions_autosomal_dominant_4\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:255237:def: \"Oculoauricular syndrome, Schorderet type is a rare, genetic developmental defect during embryogenesis characterized by various ophthalmic anomalies (including congenital microphthalmia, microcornea, cataract, anterior segment dysgenesis, ocular coloboma and early onset rod-cone dystrophy), and abnormal external ears (low-set pinna with crumpled helix, narrow intertragic incisure, abnormal bridge connecting the crus of the helix and the anthelix, narrow external acoustic meatus, and lobule aplasia).\" [Orphanet:157962]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:255247:synonym: \"microphthalmia, microcornea, anterior segment dysgenesis, cataract, ocular coloboma, retinal pigment epithelium abnormalities, Rod-cone dystrophy, and anomalies of the external Ear\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:262471:name: progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 5\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:262472:def: \"Any progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions in which the cause of the disease is a mutation in the RRM2B gene.\" [MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:262477:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions caused by mutation in RRM2B\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:262478:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 5\" EXACT [MONDO:Lexical, OMIM:613077]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:262479:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant type 5\" EXACT [MONDO:RULE_1]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:262480:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia, autosomal dominant 5\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:262481:synonym: \"RRM2B progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern, MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:262488:is_a: MONDO:0008003 ! autosomal dominant progressive external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:262489:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia_with_mitochondrial_dna_deletions_autosomal_dominant_5\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:270012:def: \"A rare, genetic, developmental defect during embryogenesis disorder characterized by severe, early-onset, salt-wasting adrenal insufficiency and ambiguous/female external genitalia (irrespective of chromosomal sex) due to mutations in the <i>CYP11A1</i> gene. Milder cases may present delayed onset of adrenal gland dysfunction and genitalia phenotype may range from normal male to female in individuals with 46,XY karyotype. Imaging studies reveal hypoplastic/absent adrenal glands and biochemical findings include low serum cortisol, mineralocorticoids, androgens, and sodium, with elevated potassium levels.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5208-3432, Orphanet:168558]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:286102:def: \"Progressive external ophthalmoplegia-myopathy-emaciation syndrome is a rare mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation disorder due to nuclear DNA anomalies characterized by progressive external ophthalmoplegia without diplopia, cerebellar atrophy, proximal skeletal muscle weakness with generalized muscle wasting, profound emaciation, respiratory failure, spinal deformity and facial muscle weakness (manifesting with ptosis, dysphonia, dysphagia and nasal speech). Intellectual disability, gastrointestinal symptoms (e.g. nausea, abdominal fullness, and loss of appetite), dilated cardiomyopathy and renal colic have also been reported.\" [Orphanet:352447]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:286118:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia-myopathy-emaciation syndrome\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:286663:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 6\" RELATED [MONDO:Lexical]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:286664:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant type 6\" EXACT [MONDO:RULE_1]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:286665:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia, autosomal dominant 6\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:286672:is_a: MONDO:0000090 ! progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:286674:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia_with_mitochondrial_dna_deletions_autosomal_dominant_6_2\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:291137:def: \"Syndromic microphthalmia-12 is a rare disease characterized by bilateral small eyeballs (microphthalmia), lungs that are too small (pulmonary hypoplasia), and a defect or hole in the diaphragm that allows the abdominal contents to move into the chest cavity (diaphragmatic hernia). Other symptoms may include: Severe global developmental delay with progressive motor impairment due to spasticity and/or uncontrolled repetitive muscular contractions (dystonia), with or without abnormal quick movements that resemble dancing (chorea), Defects of the cerebellum (Chiari type I malformation) Accumulation of cerebrospinal fluid inside the brain (hydrocephaly), Severe feeding difficulties, Mild facial dysmorphism with broad nasal root and tip, and a very small chin (micrognathia), Severe language delay, Wheelchair-bound. Syndromic microphthalmia-12 is caused by mutations in the RARB gene. There is no specific treatment for this syndrome.\" [https://rarediseases.info.nih.gov/diseases/13235/syndromic-microphthalmia-12]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:302863:name: progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 2\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:302864:def: \"Any progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions in which the cause of the disease is a mutation in the RNASEH1 gene.\" [MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:302870:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions caused by mutation in RNASEH1\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:302871:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 2\" EXACT [MONDO:Lexical, OMIM:616479]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:302872:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive type 2\" EXACT [MONDO:RULE_1]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:302873:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia, autosomal recessive 2\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:302874:synonym: \"RNASEH1 progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern, MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:302880:is_a: MONDO:0000090 ! progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:302881:is_a: MONDO:0018002 ! adult-onset chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial myopathy\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:302882:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia_with_mitochondrial_dna_deletions_autosomal_recessive_2\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:304609:def: \"Autosomal dominant optic atrophy plus syndrome (ADOA plus) is a variant of autosomal dominant optic atrophy (ADOA) associating the typical optic atrophy with other extra-ocular manifestations such as sensorineural deafness, myopathy, chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia, ataxia and peripheral neuropathy. More rarely, other manifestations have been associated with this condition, such as spastic paraplegia, multiple-sclerosis like illness.\" [Orphanet:1215]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309073:name: progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 3\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309074:def: \"Any autosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia in which the cause of the disease is a mutation in the TK2 gene.\" [MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309077:synonym: \"autosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia caused by mutation in TK2\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309079:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 3\" EXACT [OMIM:617069]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309080:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 3; PEOB3\" EXACT []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309081:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive type 3\" EXACT [MONDO:RULE_1]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309082:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia, autosomal recessive 3\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309083:synonym: \"TK2 autosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern, MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309089:is_a: MONDO:0000090 ! progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309090:is_a: MONDO:0016810 ! autosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309091:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia_with_mitochondrial_dna_deletions_autosomal_recessive_3\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309099:name: progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 4\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309100:def: \"An extremely rare multiple mitochondrial DNA deletion syndrome with markedly decreased deoxyguanosine kinase (DGUOK) activity in skeletal muscle characterized by a highly variable phenotype. Clinical manifestations include progressive external ophthalmoplegia, mitochondrial myopathy, recurrent rhabdomyolysis, lower motor neuron disease, mild cognitive impairment, sensory axonal neuropathy, optic atrophy, ataxia, hypogonadism and/or parkinsonism.\" [Orphanet:329314]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309111:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 4\" EXACT [OMIM:617070]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309112:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive type 4\" EXACT [MONDO:RULE_1]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309113:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia, autosomal recessive 4\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309121:is_a: MONDO:0000090 ! progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:309123:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia_with_mitochondrial_dna_deletions_autosomal_recessive_4\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:315529:def: \"Arachnodactyly-intellectual disability-dysmorphism syndrome is characterized by moderate intellectual deficit, brachycephaly, typical facies (thin lips and microstomia), ectomorphic habitus with extremely long, thin fingers and toes, and hypoplastic external genitalia. It has been described in three patients.\" [Orphanet:1130]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:315576:def: \"Arrhinia is an extremely rare, major congenital malformation consisting of an absence of the nose ranging from hyporrhinia (absence of external nasal structures) to total arrhinia (absence of external nose, nasal airways, olfactory bulbs, or olfactory nerve) often causing respiratory distress and requiring surgical correction. Arrhinia can be bilateral or unilateral (hemiarrhinia). Associated anomalies include ocular features (hypertelorism, microphthalmia, eyelid coloboma), facial clefts, midline defects and microtia.\" [Orphanet:1134]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:316888:def: \"Maternally inherited cardiomyopathy and hearing loss is a mitochondrial disease described in two unrelated families to date that has a heterogeneous clinical presentation characterized by the association of progressive sensorineural hearing loss with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and, in the majority of cases, encephalomyopathy symptoms such as ataxia, slurred speech, progressive external opthalmoparesis (PEO), muscle weakness, myalgia, and exercise intolerance.\" [Orphanet:1349]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:319033:def: \"Proboscis lateralis (PL) is a rare congenital facial abnormality characterized by failed development of the external nose on one side that is replaced by a tubular structure composed of skin and soft tissue usually attached at the inner canthus of the eye and therefore often associated with maldevelopment of the nasal cavity or paranasal sinuses of the affected side. PL is also associated with other craniofacial abnormalities such as orbital anomalies, cleft lip/palate, frontal encephalocele and holoprosencephaly.\" [Orphanet:141099]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:322987:def: \"Isolated congenital auditory ossicle malformation is a rare, congential, middle ear anomaly characterized by, usually unilateral and sporadic, variations in the number, size and/or configuration of the ossicles, with no tympanic membrane and external ear abnormalities and no history of trauma or infection. Patients frequently present late, after schooling has started, with non- progressive, conductive hearing loss often associated with speech delay and poor school performance.\" [Orphanet:162526]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:322994:synonym: \"congenital auditory ossicle malformation without external ear abnormality\" EXACT [Orphanet:162526]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:328429:def: \"Complete septate uterus is a rare, non-syndromic uterovaginal malformation characterized by a uterus that has a longitudinal septum which elongates from the uterine fundus to the internal or external cervical os. Most often women are asymptomatic, however dysmenorrhoea, unilateral obstruction, and endometriosis could be observed. Unlike urinary tract abnormalities, which are very rarely associated, poor reproductive outcome is frequent.\" [Orphanet:180126]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:329615:def: \"Axial mesodermal dysplasia spectrum is a rare developmental defect during embryogenesis syndrome characterized by congenital manifestations of both oculo-auriculo-vertebral spectrum and caudal regression sequence. Phenotype is highly variable but patients typically present facial dysmorphism (incl. asymmetry, hypertelorism), auricular abnormalities (e.g. preauricular tags, microtia, absence of middle ear ossicles), skeletal malformations (hemivertebrae, hip dislocation, sacral agenesis/dysplasia, talipes equinovarus, flexion deformity of lower limbs), cardiac defects (dextrocardia, septal defects), renal and genitourinary anomalies (such as renal agensis/dysplasia, abnormal external genitalia, cryptorchidia), as well as anal anomalies such as anal atresia and rectovesical fistula.\" [Orphanet:1834]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:333717:synonym: \"OPCA with macular Degeneration and external ophthalmoplegia\" RELATED []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:334520:def: \"Pulmonary fibrosis - hepatic hyperplasia - bone marrow hypoplasia, also named Btrimorphic syndromeB (i.e. three (inherited) morbidities, pulmonary, hepatic and cytopenia), is a rare disease reported in 4 cases to date, manifesting with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, hepatic nodular regenerative hyperplasia leading to portal hypertension and thrombocytopenia due to bone marrow hypoplasia. The condition was associated with 100% mortality.\" [Orphanet:210136]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:336318:def: \"A rare, genetically highly heterogeneous otorhinolaryngologic disease, resulting from inner and/or middle ear or hearing nerve anomalies, typically characterized by bilateral, severe to profound hearing loss (mean sensorineural hearing impairment of 60 dB or more for 500-, 1,000-, and 2,000-Hz frequency tones in the better ear) which occurs before the onset of speech development and is not associated with visible external ear abnormalities or any other medical problems. It is usually nonprogressive and impedes oral language acquisition.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5208-3432, Orphanet:216445]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:336339:def: \"Postlingual non-syndromic genetic deafness is a rare, genetically highly heterogeneous otorhinolaryngologic disease, resulting from inner and/or middle ear or hearing nerve anomalies, typically characterized by progressive, bilateral, moderate to profound hearing loss (mean sensorineural hearing impairment equal to 40 dB or more for 500-, 1,000-, and 2,000-Hz frequency tones in the better ear) which occurs after the onset of speech development and is not associated with visible external ear abnormalities or any other medical problems. Language development is not initially significantly delayed.\" [Orphanet:216452]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:336358:def: \"A central nervous system malformation syndrome characterized by holoprosencephaly with microcephaly, abnormal eye morphology (hypotelorism, cyclopia, exophthalmos), nasal anomalies (single nostril or absent nose), and cleft lip/palate, combined with signs of caudal regression (sacral agenesis, sirenomelia with absent external genitalia).\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5208-3432, Orphanet:2165]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:339596:def: \"Isotretinoin embryopathy is an association of malformations caused by the teratogenic effect of isotretinoin, an oral synthetic vitamin A derivative, which is used to treat severe recalcitrant cystic acne. Exposure to isotretinoin during the first trimester of pregnancy has been associated with an increased risk of spontaneous abortions and severe birth defects including serious craniofacial (microcephaly, asymmetric crying facies, microphthalmia, developmental abnormalities of the external ear, ocular hypertelorism), cardio vascular (conotruncal heart defects, aortic arch abnormalities), and central nervous system (hydrocephalus, microcephaly, lissencephaly, Dandy-Walker malformation, cognitive deficit) anomalies and thymic aplasia. Isoretinoin is contraindicated during pregnancy.\" [Orphanet:2305]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:346949:def: \"Mitochondrial DNA depletion syndrome, encephalomyopathic form is a group of mitochondrial DNA maintenance syndrome diseases characterized by predominantly neuromuscular manifestations with typically infantile onset of hypotonia, lactic acidosis, psychomotor delay, progressive hyperkinetic-dystonic movement disorders, external ophtalmoplegia, sensosineural hearing loss, generalized seizures and variable renal tubular dysfunction. It may be associated with a broad range of other clinical features.\" [Orphanet:254803]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:347094:def: \"A rare, mitochondrial DNA maintenance syndrome characterized by cerebellar ataxia, sensory peripheral neuropathy, myoclonus, epilepsy, progressive cognitive impairment, late-onset ptosis and external ophthalmoplegia. Liver failure may also occur, most often in association with the use of antiepileptic drug sodium valproate.\" [Orphanet:254881]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:347120:name: autosomal recessive progressive external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:347121:def: \"Autosomal recessive form of progressive external ophthalmoplegia.\" [MONDO:patterns/autosomal_recessive]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:347129:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia, autosomal recessive\" EXACT [MONDO:patterns/autosomal_recessive]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:347135:is_a: MONDO:0005181 ! progressive external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:347137:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/autosomal_recessive_progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:353838:def: \"Machado-Joseph disease type 2 is a subtype of Machado-Joseph disease (SCA3/MJD) with intermediate severity characterized by an intermediate age of onset, cerebellar ataxia and external progressive ophthalmoplegia, with variable pyramidal and extrapyramidal signs.\" [Orphanet:276241]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:356414:def: \"Infantile glycerol kinase deficiency (GKD) is a severe form of GKD characterized clinically by poor feeding, failure to thrive, salt-wasting dehydration, vomiting, Addisonian pigmentation, hypotonia, and disorders of consciousness. Some patients have complex GKD associated with adrenal hypoplasia congenita and/or Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) with manifestations including intellectual deficit, dysmorphic facial features, abnormal external genitalia, strabismus, seizures, and progressive lethargy.\" [Orphanet:284408]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:357379:def: \"Inherited isolated adrenal insufficiency due to partial CYP11A1 deficiency is a rare, genetic, chronic, primary adrenal insufficiency disorder, due to partial loss-of-function CYP11A1 mutations, characterized by early-onset adrenal insufficiency without associated abnormal external male genitalia. Patients present with signs of adrenal crisis, including electrolite abnormalities, severe weakness, recurrent vomiting and seizures. Ultrasound reveals absent (or very small) adrenal glands.\" [Orphanet:289548]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:358658:def: \"A rare disease caused by compression of the celiac axis by an abnormally shaped arcuate ligament (the part of the diaphragm in which both pillars join in the midline around the aorta). Patients have recurrent abdominal pain, anorexia and weight loss. The pain is epigastric, and diarrhea or constipation may be present as well. Onset of pain will usually, although not always, be after food intake, and may be associated with nausea and emesis. Other symptoms may include lassitude, exercise intolerance and vomiting. Occasionally, a patient may show an abdominal murmur upon auscultation.\" [Orphanet:293208]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:361971:def: \"A syndrome characterized by the association of gastrointestinal dysmotility, peripheral neuropathy, chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia and leukoencephalopathy.\" [Orphanet:298]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:362018:def: \"Conditions affecting individuals with 46,XX karyotype characterized by atypical development of one or more of the following: the gonads, the internal reproductive structures, the external reproductive/genital structures.\" [NCIT:C127169]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:366458:def: \"A rare multiple congenital anomalies syndrome characterized by mild to severe intellectual disability, a distinctive facial gestalt (blepharophimosis, maxillary hypoplasia, telecanthus, microtia and atresia of the external auditory meatus) as well as skeletal and articular abnormalities (e.g. camptodactyly of the fingers, cutaneous syndactyly, talipes equinovarus, flexion contractures of the proximal interphalangeal joints, hip or elbow subluxation, joint laxity). Affected individuals also present neonatal hypotonia, variable respiratory manifestations, chronic feeding difficulties and gray matter heterotopia.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5208-3432, Orphanet:314679]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:367047:def: \"The salt wasting form of classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency (classical 21 OHD CAH) is characterized by virilization of the external genitalia in females, hypocortisolism, precocious pseudopuberty and renal salt loss due to aldosterone deficiency.\" [Orphanet:315306]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:367066:def: \"The simple virilizing form of classical congenital adrenal hyperplasia due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency (classical 21 OHD CAH) is characterized by genital ambiguity and virilization of the external genitalia in females, hypocortisolism and precocious pseudopuberty without salt-wasting.\" [Orphanet:315311]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:370383:name: adult-onset chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial myopathy\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:370384:def: \"Adult-onset chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial myopathy is a rare mitochondrial disease characterized by adult onset of progressive external ophthalmoplegia, exercise intolerance, muscle weakness, manifestations of spinocerebellar ataxia (e.g. impaired gait, dysarthria) and mild motor peripheral neuropathy. Respiratory insufficiency has been reported in some cases.\" [Orphanet:329336]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:375208:name: childhood-onset autosomal recessive myopathy with external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:375209:def: \"A rare, genetic, non-dystrophic myopathy disease characterized by childhood-onset severe external ophthalmoplegia, typically without ptosis, associated with mild, very slowly progressive muscular weakness and atrophy, involving the facial, neck flexor and limb (upper > lower, proximal > distal) muscles. Muscle biopsy shows type 1 fiber uniformity, absent, or abnormally small, type 2A fibers, increased variability of fiber size, internalized nuclei and/or fatty infiltration.\" [Orphanet:363677]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:375224:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/childhood_onset_autosomal_recessive_myopathy_with_external_ophthalmoplegia\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:392365:name: maternally-inherited progressive external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:392372:synonym: \"maternally-inherited chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia\" EXACT [Orphanet:663]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:392380:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/mitochondrial_dna_related_progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:393171:comment: Note that the ordo class is specifically for rare diseases, but we broaden to be inclusive of HH1\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:395647:def: \"Androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS) is a disorder of sex development (DSD) characterized by the presence of female external genitalia, ambiguous genitalia or variable defects in virilization in a 46,XY individual with absent or partial responsiveness to age-appropriate levels of androgens. It comprises two clinical subgroups: complete AIS (CAIS) and partial AIS (PAIS).\" [Orphanet:754]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:395703:def: \"A condition in males that affects sexual development. It is characterized by underdevelopment of the Leydig cells, which are cells in the testes that secrete male sex hormones (androgens) and are important for male sexual development. Individuals with LCH have a typical male genetic make-up (46, XY), but due to lowered levels of androgens, may have a range of genital (reproductive organ) differences. Individuals with LCH may have a small penis (micropenis),the opening of the urethra may be located on the underside of the penis (hypospadias), or the scrotum may be divided into two halves (bifid scrotum). Given these differences in development, the external genitalia may not appear clearly male or female (ambiguous genitalia). Some individuals with LCH can have female external genitalia and small testes that have not descended and are located in the pelvis, abdomen, or groin. This may be referred to as type 1, whereas less severe cases might be called type 2. LCH is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner and is caused by mutations in the LHCGR gene.Although there is no specific treatment or cure for LCH, there may be ways to manage the symptoms. A team of doctors or specialists is often needed to figure out the treatment options for each person.\" [GARD:0003244]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:396414:def: \"The association of auricular abnormalities and cleft lip with or without cleft palate has been described in two siblings. One sibling had postauricular pits, profound myopia, nystagmus and retinal pigment abnormalities. The second sibling was a fetus (gestational age: 23 weeks) with severe cleft lip, cleft palate and external ear abnormalities.\" [Orphanet:77300]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:396938:def: \"A rare genetic neuromuscular disease characterized by early onset of proximal or generalized muscle weakness, external ophthalmoplegia with or without ptosis, and joint contractures. Hypotonia, neonatal respiratory distress necessitating ventilation, and severe dysphagia have also been reported. The disease is of variable severity and non- or slowly progressive. Patients typically remain ambulatory. Muscle biopsy may show predominance of type 1 fibers, marked variability in fiber size, increased internal nuclei, and proliferation of perimysial and endomysial connective tissue.\" [Orphanet:79091]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:397320:def: \"Bickerstaff's brainstem encephalitis (BBE) is a rare post-infectious neurological disease characterized by the association of external ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, lower limb arreflexia, extensor plantar response and disturbance of consciousness (drowsiness, stupor or coma).\" [Orphanet:79138]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:401612:name: macrostomia-preauricular tags-external ophthalmoplegia syndrome\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:401613:def: \"Macrostomia-preauricular tags-external ophthalmoplegia syndrome combines macrostomia or abnormal mouth contour, preauricular tags, uni- or bilateral ptosis and external ophthalmoplegia. It was described in nine members of a Brazilian family. It is a new phenotype belonging to the so-called oculoauriculovertebral spectrum, resulting from a branchial arch anomaly. Transmission is autosomal dominant.\" [Orphanet:83619]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:401625:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/macrostomia_preauricular_tags_external_ophthalmoplegia_syndrome\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:401643:synonym: \"perineal hemangioma-external genitalia malformations-lipomyelomeningocele-vesicorenal abnormalities-imperforate anus syndrome\" EXACT []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:410637:def: \"A congenital malformation of the external ear and the most extreme form of microtia characterized by the complete absence of the external ear and auditory canal, conductive hearing loss, attention deficit disorders and delayed language development.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5208-3432, Orphanet:93976]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:415225:def: \"Robinow syndrome (RS) is a rare genetic syndrome characterized by limb shortening and abnormalities of the head, face and external genitalia.\" [Orphanet:97360]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:427401:name: progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 5\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:427406:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal recessive 5\" EXACT [OMIM:618098]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:427407:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia, autosomal recessive 5\" EXACT [OMIM:618098]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:427413:is_a: MONDO:0000090 ! progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:427414:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia_with_mitochondrial_dna_deletions_autosomal_recessive_5\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:427995:def: \"Hemochromatosis type 1 (classic) is the most common form of hereditary hemochromatosis (HH), a group of diseases characterized by excessive tissue iron deposition. Due to its incidence (1/200-1/1000), it is not considered as a rare disease, unlike the other subforms of the disease\" [EFO:0006513, https://github.com/EBISPOT/efo/issues/58]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:428489:def: \"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (CAIS) is a form of androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS), a disorder of sex development (DSD), characterized by the presence of female external genitalia in a 46,XY individual with normal testis development but undescended testes and unresponsiveness to age-appropriate levels of androgens.\" [Orphanet:99429]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:431220:def: \"Damage inflicted on the body as the direct or indirect result of an external force, with or without disruption of structural continuity.\" [NCIT:C3671]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432154:name: external ear neoplasm\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432155:def: \"A neoplasm (disease) that involves the external ear.\" [MONDO:patterns/location]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432156:synonym: \"external ear neoplasm (disease)\" EXACT [MONDO:patterns/location]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432157:synonym: \"external Ear tumor\" EXACT [NCIT:C4652]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432158:synonym: \"external ear tumor\" EXACT [MONDO:patterns/neoplasm, NCIT:C4652]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432159:synonym: \"external Ear tumour\" EXACT OMO:0003005 []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432160:synonym: \"external ear tumour\" EXACT OMO:0003005 []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432161:synonym: \"neoplasm of external Ear\" EXACT [NCIT:C4652]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432162:synonym: \"neoplasm of external ear\" EXACT [MONDO:patterns/neoplasm, NCIT:C4652]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432163:synonym: \"neoplasm of the external Ear\" EXACT [NCIT:C4652]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432164:synonym: \"tumor of external Ear\" EXACT [NCIT:C4652]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432165:synonym: \"tumor of external ear\" EXACT [MONDO:patterns/neoplasm, NCIT:C4652]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432166:synonym: \"tumor of the external Ear\" EXACT [NCIT:C4652]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432167:synonym: \"tumour of external Ear\" EXACT OMO:0003005 []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432168:synonym: \"tumour of external ear\" EXACT OMO:0003005 []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432169:synonym: \"tumour of the external Ear\" EXACT OMO:0003005 []\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:432174:is_a: MONDO:0002776 ! external ear disorder\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:437420:def: \"A viral or bacterial infection that affects the external, middle, or inner ear. It may follow an upper respiratory infection. Signs and symptoms include pain, ear discharge, ear fullness, hearing loss, vertigo, nausea, and vomiting.\" [NCIT:C27193]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:445058:name: progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 1\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:445059:def: \"Any autosomal dominant progressive external ophthalmoplegia in which the cause of the disease is a mutation in the POLG gene.\" [MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:445063:synonym: \"autosomal dominant progressive external ophthalmoplegia caused by mutation in POLG\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:445065:synonym: \"POLG autosomal dominant progressive external ophthalmoplegia\" EXACT [MONDO:design_pattern, MONDO:patterns/disease_series_by_gene]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:445066:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions, autosomal dominant 1\" EXACT [OMIM:157640]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:445067:synonym: \"progressive external ophthalmoplegia, autosomal dominant 1\" EXACT [OMIM:157640]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:445073:is_a: MONDO:0008003 ! autosomal dominant progressive external ophthalmoplegia\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:467939:def: \"Choanal atresia-athelia-hypothyroidism-delayed puberty-short stature syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by choanal atresia, athelia or hypoplastic nipples, branchial sinus abnormalities, neck pits, lacrimal duct anomalies, hearing loss, external ear malformations, and thyroid abnormalities.\" [OMIM:620186]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:473389:def: \"Penetrating and non-penetrating injuries to the spinal cord resulting from traumatic external forces (e.g., WOUNDS, GUNSHOT; WHIPLASH INJURIES; etc.).\" [MESH:D013119]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:476043:name: X-linked external auditory canal atresia-dilated internal auditory canal-facial dysmorphism syndrome\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:485469:def: \"46,XX testicular disorder of sex development (46,XX testicular DSD) is characterized by male external genitalia, ranging from normal to ambiguous with associated testosterone deficiency.\" [Orphanet:393]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:491516:def: \"An anterior midline defect with variable expression involving the infraumbilical abdominal wall including the pelvis, urinary tract, and external genitalia.\" [OMIM:600057]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:494239:def: \"A rare disease due to a severely impaired central autonomic control of breathing and dysfunction of the autonomous nervous system. The incidence is estimated to be at 1 of 200 000 livebirths. A heterozygous mutation of PHOX-2B gene is found in 90% of the patients. Association with a Hirschsprung's disease is observed in 16% of the cases. Despite a high mortality rate and a lifelong dependence to mechanical ventilation, the long-term outcome of CCHS should be ultimately improved by multidisciplinary and coordinated follow-up of the patients.\" [Orphanet:661]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:499535:def: \"A myopathy that is characterized by muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis), heat and exertion-related muscle pain (myalgia) and cramping symptoms, severe muscle pain, sudden elevation and subsequent fall of serum creatine phosphokinase levels and products of muscle breakdown in the urine (myoglobinuria). Associated with RYR1 variations. Rhabdomyolysis is associated with a range of external triggers, including strenuous exercise beyond the limit of fatigue, heat stress, illicit drug or alcohol abuse, use of supplements or certain medications, recent viral illness or muscle trauma.\" [DOID:0080992]\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:505226:name: progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial dna deletions, autosomal recessive 6\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:505233:is_a: MONDO:0000090 ! progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-rare.obo:505234:property_value: curated_content_resource \"https://www.malacards.org/card/progressive_external_ophthalmoplegia_with_mitochondrial_dna_deletions_autosomal_recessive_6\" xsd:anyURI {source=\"MONDO:MalaCards\"}\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:357:        <rdfs:label rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Synonym to be removed from public release but maintained in edit version as record of external usage</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:419:        <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">GARD rare disease subset</rdfs:comment>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:62972:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A cell within an epithelial cell sheet whose main function is to act as an internal or external covering for a tissue or an organism.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:62980:        <owl:annotatedTarget>A cell within an epithelial cell sheet whose main function is to act as an internal or external covering for a tissue or an organism.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:66815:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A cell of a layer of transitional epithelium in the wall of the bladder, ureter, and renal pelvis, external to the lamina propria.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:66838:        <owl:annotatedTarget>A cell of a layer of transitional epithelium in the wall of the bladder, ureter, and renal pelvis, external to the lamina propria.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:86574:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A layer of some material entity which is adjacent to one or more of its external boundaries and directly interacts with its immediate surroundings.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:99196:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A cellular homeostatic process involved in the maintenance of an internal steady state of glucose within a cell or between a cell and its external environment.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:100624:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The movement of an immune cell in response to an external stimulus contributing to an inflammatory response.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:100649:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The movement of an immune cell in response to an external stimulus a part of an immune response.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:101301:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An immune response-regulating cell surface receptor signaling pathway that contributes to the endocytic engulfment of external particulate material by phagocytes.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:107268:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The space external to the outermost structure of a cell. For cells without external protective or external encapsulating structures this refers to space outside of the plasma membrane. This term covers the host cell environment outside an intracellular parasite.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:107448:        <obo:IAO_0000115>OBSOLETE. The basic structural and functional unit of all organisms. Includes the plasma membrane and any external encapsulating structures such as the cell wall and cell envelope.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:107910:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The membrane surrounding a cell that separates the cell from its external environment. It consists of a phospholipid bilayer and associated proteins.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:110518:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A vesicle-mediated transport process in which cells take up external materials or membrane constituents by the invagination of a small region of the plasma membrane to form a new membrane-bounded vesicle.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:110589:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A vesicle-mediated transport process that results in the engulfment of external particulate material by phagocytes and their delivery to the lysosome. The particles are initially contained within phagocytic vacuoles (phagosomes), which then fuse with primary lysosomes to effect digestion of the particles.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:110674:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A programmed cell death process which begins when a cell receives an internal (e.g. DNA damage) or external signal (e.g. an extracellular death ligand), and proceeds through a series of biochemical events (signaling pathway phase) which trigger an execution phase. The execution phase is the last step of an apoptotic process, and is typically characterized by rounding-up of the cell, retraction of pseudopodes, reduction of cellular volume (pyknosis), chromatin condensation, nuclear fragmentation (karyorrhexis), plasma membrane blebbing and fragmentation of the cell into apoptotic bodies. When the execution phase is completed, the cell has died.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:110730:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The directed, self-propelled movement of a cell or subcellular component without the involvement of an external agent such as a transporter or a pore.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:111962:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The internally coordinated responses (actions or inactions) of animals (individuals or groups) to internal or external stimuli, via a mechanism that involves nervous system activity.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:113796:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The series of events in which an external stimulus is received by a cell and converted into a molecular signal.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:113797:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>perception of external stimulus</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:113798:        <rdfs:label>detection of external stimulus</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:113844:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that results in a change in state or activity of a cell or an organism (in terms of movement, secretion, enzyme production, gene expression, etc.) as a result of an external stimulus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:113846:        <rdfs:label>response to external stimulus</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:117203:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A process which begins when a cell receives an internal or external signal and activates a series of biochemical events (signaling pathway). The process ends with the death of the cell.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:122108:        <rdfs:label>external encapsulating structure</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:122377:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The directed movement of a neutrophil cell, the most numerous polymorphonuclear leukocyte found in the blood, in response to an external stimulus, usually an infection or wounding.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:122404:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The movement of a leukocyte in response to an external stimulus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:123144:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A structure lying external to one or more cells, which provides structural support, biochemical or biomechanical cues for cells or tissues.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125093:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that modulates the frequency, rate or extent of a response to an external stimulus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125094:        <rdfs:label>regulation of response to external stimulus</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125121:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that stops, prevents, or reduces the frequency, rate or extent of a response to an external stimulus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125122:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>down regulation of response to external stimulus</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125123:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>down-regulation of response to external stimulus</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125124:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>downregulation of response to external stimulus</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125125:        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>inhibition of response to external stimulus</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125126:        <rdfs:label>negative regulation of response to external stimulus</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125153:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that activates, maintains or increases the rate of a response to an external stimulus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125154:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>up regulation of response to external stimulus</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125155:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>up-regulation of response to external stimulus</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125156:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>upregulation of response to external stimulus</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125157:        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>activation of response to external stimulus</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125158:        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>stimulation of response to external stimulus</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:125159:        <rdfs:label>positive regulation of response to external stimulus</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:130828:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The directed movement of a motile cell or organism in response to an external stimulus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:132189:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A process that is carried out at the cellular level which results in the assembly, arrangement of constituent parts, or disassembly of structures in the space external to the outermost structure of a cell. For cells without external protective or external encapsulating structures this refers to space outside of the plasma membrane, and also covers the host cell environment outside an intracellular parasite.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:132667:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that results in a change in state or activity of a cell or an organism (in terms of movement, secretion, enzyme production, gene expression, etc.) as a result of an external biotic stimulus, an external stimulus caused by, or produced by living things.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:132668:        <rdfs:label>response to external biotic stimulus</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:133589:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The process whose specific outcome is the progression of the skin over time, from its formation to the mature structure. The skin is the external membranous integument of an animal. In vertebrates the skin generally consists of two layers, an outer nonsensitive and nonvascular epidermis (cuticle or skarfskin) composed of cells which are constantly growing and multiplying in the deeper, and being thrown off in the superficial layers, as well as an inner vascular dermis (cutis, corium or true skin) composed mostly of connective tissue.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:134476:        <obo:IAO_0000115>OBSOLETE. Any constituent part of the extracellular matrix, the structure lying external to one or more cells, which provides structural support for cells or tissues; may be completely external to the cell (as in animals) or be part of the cell (as often seen in plants).</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:134488:        <obo:IAO_0000115>OBSOLETE. Any constituent part of the extracellular region, the space external to the outermost structure of a cell. For cells without external protective or external encapsulating structures this refers to space outside of the plasma membrane. This term covers constituent parts of the host cell environment outside an intracellular parasite.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:134755:        <obo:IAO_0000115>OBSOLETE. Any constituent part of the plasma membrane, the membrane surrounding a cell that separates the cell from its external environment. It consists of a phospholipid bilayer and associated proteins.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:135353:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A process that is carried out at the cellular level which results in the assembly, arrangement of constituent parts, or disassembly of external structures that lie outside the plasma membrane and surround the entire cell.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:135354:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external encapsulating structure organisation</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:135355:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external encapsulating structure organization and biogenesis</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:135356:        <rdfs:label>external encapsulating structure organization</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:141841:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that modulates the frequency, rate or extent of receptor mediated endocytosis, the uptake of external materials by cells, utilizing receptors to ensure specificity of transport.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:141870:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that activates or increases the frequency, rate or extent of receptor mediated endocytosis, the uptake of external materials by cells, utilizing receptors to ensure specificity of transport.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:141904:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that stops, prevents, or reduces the frequency, rate or extent of receptor mediated endocytosis, the uptake of external materials by cells, utilizing receptors to ensure specificity of transport.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:142146:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that activates or increases the frequency, rate or extent of behavior, the internally coordinated responses (actions or inactions) of whole living organisms (individuals or groups) to internal or external stimuli.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:142179:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that stops, prevents, or reduces the frequency, rate or extent of behavior, the internally coordinated responses (actions or inactions) of whole living organisms (individuals or groups) to internal or external stimuli.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:143284:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The multiplication or reproduction of epithelial cells, resulting in the expansion of a cell population. Epithelial cells make up the epithelium, the covering of internal and external surfaces of the body, including the lining of vessels and other small cavities. It consists of cells joined by small amounts of cementing substances.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:143695:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that modulates the frequency, rate or extent of phagocytosis, the process in which phagocytes engulf external particulate material.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:144213:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that modulates the frequency, rate or extent of behavior, the internally coordinated responses (actions or inactions) of whole living organisms (individuals or groups) to internal or external stimuli.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:145195:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The series of events required for an organism to receive a touch stimulus, convert it to a molecular signal, and recognize and characterize the signal. This is a neurological process. The perception of touch in animals is mediated by mechanoreceptors in the skin and mucous membranes and is the sense by which contact with objects gives evidence as to certain of their qualities. Different types of touch can be perceived (for example, light, coarse, pressure and tickling) and the stimulus may be external or internal (e.g. the feeling of a full stomach).</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:146144:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that modulates the frequency, rate or extent of a process involved in the formation, arrangement of constituent parts, or disassembly of cell structures, including the plasma membrane and any external encapsulating structures such as the cell wall and cell envelope.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:146176:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that stops, prevents, or reduces the frequency, rate or extent of a process involved in the formation, arrangement of constituent parts, or disassembly of cell structures, including the plasma membrane and any external encapsulating structures such as the cell wall and cell envelope.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:146210:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that activates or increases the frequency, rate or extent of a process involved in the formation, arrangement of constituent parts, or disassembly of cell structures, including the plasma membrane and any external encapsulating structures such as the cell wall and cell envelope.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:150281:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The process whose specific outcome is the progression of the pharyngeal system over time, from its formation to the mature structure. The pharyngeal system is a transient embryonic complex that is specific to vertebrates. It comprises the pharyngeal arches, bulges of tissues of mesoderm and neural crest derivation through which pass nerves and pharyngeal arch arteries. The arches are separated internally by pharyngeal pouches, evaginations of foregut endoderm, and externally by pharyngeal clefts, invaginations of surface ectoderm. The development of the system ends when the stucture it contributes to are forming: the thymus, thyroid, parathyroids, maxilla, mandible, aortic arch, cardiac outflow tract, external and middle ear.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:151353:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The process whose specific outcome is the progression of an epithelium over time, from its formation to the mature structure. An epithelium is a tissue that covers the internal or external surfaces of an anatomical structure.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:154494:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that results in a change in state or activity of a cell (in terms of movement, secretion, enzyme production, gene expression, etc.) as a result of an external stimulus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:154495:        <rdfs:label>cellular response to external stimulus</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:154505:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The movement of a granulocyte in response to an external stimulus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:154532:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that modulates the rate, frequency or extent of granulocyte chemotaxis. Granulocyte chemotaxis is the movement of a granulocyte in response to an external stimulus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:154560:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that decreases the rate, frequency or extent of granulocyte chemotaxis. Granulocyte chemotaxis is the movement of a granulocyte in response to an external stimulus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:154588:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that increases the rate, frequency or extent of granulocyte chemotaxis. Granulocyte chemotaxis is the movement of a granulocyte in response to an external stimulus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:154934:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The part of a cell encompassing the cell cortex, the plasma membrane, and any external encapsulating structures.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:155503:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that modulates the frequency, rate, or extent of neutrophil chemotaxis. Neutrophil chemotaxis is the directed movement of a neutrophil cell, the most numerous polymorphonuclear leukocyte found in the blood, in response to an external stimulus, usually an infection or wounding.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:155532:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that increases the frequency, rate, or extent of neutrophil chemotaxis. Neutrophil chemotaxis is the directed movement of a neutrophil cell, the most numerous polymorphonuclear leukocyte found in the blood, in response to an external stimulus, usually an infection or wounding.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:155561:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any process that decreases the frequency, rate, or extent of neutrophil chemotaxis. Neutrophil chemotaxis is the directed movement of a neutrophil cell, the most numerous polymorphonuclear leukocyte found in the blood, in response to an external stimulus, usually an infection or wounding.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:156432:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Morphogenesis of a sensory organ. A sensory organ is defined as a tissue or set of tissues that work together to receive and transmit signals from external or internal stimuli. Morphogenesis is the process in which anatomical structures are generated and organized. Organs are commonly observed as visibly distinct structures, but may also exist as loosely associated clusters of cells that work together to perform a specific function or functions.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:157022:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The binding by a cell-adhesion protein on a cell surface to an adhesion molecule on another cell surface, to mediate adhesion of the cell to the external substrate or to another cell.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:159572:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A vesicle-mediated transport process in which the presynapse take up external materials or membrane constituents by the invagination of a small region of the plasma membrane to form a new membrane-bounded vesicle.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:159601:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A vesicle-mediated transport process in which the postsynapse take up external materials or membrane constituents by the invagination of a small region of the plasma membrane to form a new membrane-bounded vesicle.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:176999:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An abnormality of male external genitalia.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:177003:        <rdfs:label>Abnormality of male external genitalia</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:177008:        <owl:annotatedTarget>An abnormality of male external genitalia.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:177046:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Abnormality of the male external sex organ.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:177066:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Hermaphroditism refers to a discrepancy between the morphology of the gonads and that of the external genitalia. In male pseudohermaphroditism, the genotype is male (XY) and the external genitalia are imcompletely virilized, ambiguous, or complete female. If gonads are present, they are testes.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:177077:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Hermaphroditism refers to a discrepancy between the morphology of the gonads and that of the external genitalia. In male pseudohermaphroditism, the genotype is male (XY) and the external genitalia are imcompletely virilized, ambiguous, or complete female. If gonads are present, they are testes.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:177116:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Underdevelopment of part or all of the male external reproductive organs (which include the penis, the scrotum and the urethra).</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:177121:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>Small male external genitalia</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:177125:        <rdfs:label>Hypoplastic male external genitalia</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:177130:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Underdevelopment of part or all of the male external reproductive organs (which include the penis, the scrotum and the urethra).</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:177136:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Small male external genitalia</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:180356:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Tinnitus is an auditory perception that can be described as the experience of sound, in the ear or in the head, in the absence of external acoustic stimulation.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:180372:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Tinnitus is an auditory perception that can be described as the experience of sound, in the ear or in the head, in the absence of external acoustic stimulation.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:183681:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>Abnormal external genitalia</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:183684:        <rdfs:label>Abnormal external genitalia</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:183689:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Abnormal external genitalia</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:186602:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Complete absence of wakefulness and content of conscience, which manifests itself as a lack of response to any kind of external stimuli.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:186616:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Complete absence of wakefulness and content of conscience, which manifests itself as a lack of response to any kind of external stimuli.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:189735:        <rdfs:comment>The vestibular apparatus is the nonauditory portion of the inner ear that mediates the subjective sensation of motion and spatial orientation of the head, adjusts muscular activity and body position to maintain posture, and stabilizes in space the fixation point of the eyes when the head moves, in order to provide a stable image upon the retina. The functioning of the vestibular system is often checked clinically by means of the caloric test, in which the head is tilted backward by about 60 degrees and either warm or cold water is introduced into the external auditory meatus on one side. The immediate results are usually vertigo, nausea, nystagmus, and twisting of the head and body.</rdfs:comment>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:197800:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Underdevelopment of part or all of the external reproductive organs.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:197806:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>Underdevelopment of external reproductive organs</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:197815:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Underdevelopment of part or all of the external reproductive organs.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:197821:        <owl:annotatedTarget>Underdevelopment of external reproductive organs</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:198983:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>Abnormality of external features of the abdomen</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:203778:        <rdfs:comment>Abnormality of the female internal or external genitalia.</rdfs:comment>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:243783:        <obo:IAO_0000115 rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Diseases of the ninth cranial (glossopharyngeal) nerve or its nuclei in the medulla. The nerve may be injured by diseases affecting the lower brain stem, floor of the posterior fossa, jugular foramen, or the nerve&apos;s extracranial course. Clinical manifestations include loss of sensation from the pharynx, decreased salivation, and syncope. Glossopharyngeal neuralgia refers to a condition that features recurrent unilateral sharp pain in the tongue, angle of the jaw, external auditory meatus and throat that may be associated with syncope. Episodes may be triggered by cough, sneeze, swallowing, or pressure on the tragus of the ear. (Adams et al., Principles of Neurology, 6th ed, p1390)</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:243807:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Diseases of the ninth cranial (glossopharyngeal) nerve or its nuclei in the medulla. The nerve may be injured by diseases affecting the lower brain stem, floor of the posterior fossa, jugular foramen, or the nerve&apos;s extracranial course. Clinical manifestations include loss of sensation from the pharynx, decreased salivation, and syncope. Glossopharyngeal neuralgia refers to a condition that features recurrent unilateral sharp pain in the tongue, angle of the jaw, external auditory meatus and throat that may be associated with syncope. Episodes may be triggered by cough, sneeze, swallowing, or pressure on the tragus of the ear. (Adams et al., Principles of Neurology, 6th ed, p1390)</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:262609:        <obo:IAO_0000115 rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">An acute neurological disorder characterized by the triad of ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and disturbances of mental activity or consciousness. Eye movement abnormalities include nystagmus, external rectus palsies, and reduced conjugate gaze. thiamine deficiency and chronic alcoholism are associated conditions. Pathologic features include periventricular petechial hemorrhages and neuropil breakdown in the diencephalon and brainstem. Chronic thiamine deficiency may lead to korsakoff syndrome. (Adams et al., Principles of Neurology, 6th ed, pp1139-42; Davis &amp; Robertson, Textbook of Neuropathology, 2nd ed, pp452-3)</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:262637:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">An acute neurological disorder characterized by the triad of ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and disturbances of mental activity or consciousness. Eye movement abnormalities include nystagmus, external rectus palsies, and reduced conjugate gaze. thiamine deficiency and chronic alcoholism are associated conditions. Pathologic features include periventricular petechial hemorrhages and neuropil breakdown in the diencephalon and brainstem. Chronic thiamine deficiency may lead to korsakoff syndrome. (Adams et al., Principles of Neurology, 6th ed, pp1139-42; Davis &amp; Robertson, Textbook of Neuropathology, 2nd ed, pp452-3)</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:265924:        <obo:IAO_0000115 rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Spinocerebellar ataxia type 3 (SCA3), also known as Machado-Joseph disease, is the most common subtype of type 1 autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia (ADCA type 1), a neurodegenerative disorder, and is characterized by ataxia, external progressive ophthalmoplegia, and other neurological manifestations.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:265981:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Spinocerebellar ataxia type 3 (SCA3), also known as Machado-Joseph disease, is the most common subtype of type 1 autosomal dominant cerebellar ataxia (ADCA type 1), a neurodegenerative disorder, and is characterized by ataxia, external progressive ophthalmoplegia, and other neurological manifestations.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:282988:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">OPCA with macular Degeneration and external ophthalmoplegia</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:283157:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">OPCA with macular Degeneration and external ophthalmoplegia</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:288817:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">microcephaly, IUGR, hypertelorism, ptosis, iris coloboma, hooked nose, external ear dysplasia, psychomotor retardation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:289070:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">microcephaly, IUGR, hypertelorism, ptosis, iris coloboma, hooked nose, external ear dysplasia, psychomotor retardation</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:293671:        <obo:IAO_0000115 rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">A rare disease due to a severely impaired central autonomic control of breathing and dysfunction of the autonomous nervous system. The incidence is estimated to be at 1 of 200 000 livebirths. A heterozygous mutation of PHOX-2B gene is found in 90% of the patients. Association with a Hirschsprung&apos;s disease is observed in 16% of the cases. Despite a high mortality rate and a lifelong dependence to mechanical ventilation, the long-term outcome of CCHS should be ultimately improved by multidisciplinary and coordinated follow-up of the patients.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:293726:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">A rare disease due to a severely impaired central autonomic control of breathing and dysfunction of the autonomous nervous system. The incidence is estimated to be at 1 of 200 000 livebirths. A heterozygous mutation of PHOX-2B gene is found in 90% of the patients. Association with a Hirschsprung&apos;s disease is observed in 16% of the cases. Despite a high mortality rate and a lifelong dependence to mechanical ventilation, the long-term outcome of CCHS should be ultimately improved by multidisciplinary and coordinated follow-up of the patients.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:349231:        <obo:IAO_0000115 rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Kearns-Sayre syndrome (KSS) is a mitochondrial disease characterized by progressive external ophthalmoplegia (PEO), pigmentary retinitis and an onset before the age of 20 years. Common additional features include deafness, cerebellar ataxia and heart block.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:349249:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia with myopathy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:349254:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">ophthalmoplegia, progressive external, with ragged red fibers</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:349255:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">ophthalmoplegia, progressive external, with ragged-Red fibers</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:349288:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Kearns-Sayre syndrome (KSS) is a mitochondrial disease characterized by progressive external ophthalmoplegia (PEO), pigmentary retinitis and an onset before the age of 20 years. Common additional features include deafness, cerebellar ataxia and heart block.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:349417:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia with myopathy</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:349447:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">ophthalmoplegia, progressive external, with ragged red fibers</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:349453:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">ophthalmoplegia, progressive external, with ragged-Red fibers</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:380712:        <obo:IAO_0000115 rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Sensory ataxic neuropathy-dysarthria-ophthalmoparesis syndrome is characterised by adult-onset severe sensory ataxic neuropathy, dysarthria and chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:380784:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Sensory ataxic neuropathy-dysarthria-ophthalmoparesis syndrome is characterised by adult-onset severe sensory ataxic neuropathy, dysarthria and chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:398543:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">external ophthalmoplegia with synergistic divergence</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:398544:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">external ophthalmoplegia, synergistic divergence, jaw winking, and oculocutaneous hypopigmentation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:398586:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">external ophthalmoplegia with synergistic divergence</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:398592:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">external ophthalmoplegia, synergistic divergence, jaw winking, and oculocutaneous hypopigmentation</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:462862:        <obo:IAO_0000115 rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Autosomal dominant optic atrophy plus syndrome (ADOA plus) is a variant of autosomal dominant optic atrophy (ADOA) associating the typical optic atrophy with other extra-ocular manifestations such as sensorineural deafness, myopathy, chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia, ataxia and peripheral neuropathy. More rarely, other manifestations have been associated with this condition, such as spastic paraplegia, multiple-sclerosis like illness.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:462903:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Autosomal dominant optic atrophy plus syndrome (ADOA plus) is a variant of autosomal dominant optic atrophy (ADOA) associating the typical optic atrophy with other extra-ocular manifestations such as sensorineural deafness, myopathy, chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia, ataxia and peripheral neuropathy. More rarely, other manifestations have been associated with this condition, such as spastic paraplegia, multiple-sclerosis like illness.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:496208:        <obo:IAO_0000115 rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Machado-Joseph disease type 2 is a subtype of Machado-Joseph disease (SCA3/MJD) with intermediate severity characterized by an intermediate age of onset, cerebellar ataxia and external progressive ophthalmoplegia, with variable pyramidal and extrapyramidal signs.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:496232:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Machado-Joseph disease type 2 is a subtype of Machado-Joseph disease (SCA3/MJD) with intermediate severity characterized by an intermediate age of onset, cerebellar ataxia and external progressive ophthalmoplegia, with variable pyramidal and extrapyramidal signs.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:499759:        <obo:IAO_0000115 rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Mitochondrial NeuroGastroIntestinal Encephalomyopathy (MNGIE) syndrome is characterized by the association of gastrointestinal dysmotility, peripheral neuropathy, chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia and leukoencephalopathy.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:499803:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Mitochondrial NeuroGastroIntestinal Encephalomyopathy (MNGIE) syndrome is characterized by the association of gastrointestinal dysmotility, peripheral neuropathy, chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia and leukoencephalopathy.</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:521827:        <obo:IAO_0000115 rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Bickerstaff&apos;s brainstem encephalitis (BBE) is a rare post-infectious neurological disease characterized by the association of external ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, lower limb arreflexia, extensor plantar response and disturbance of consciousness (drowsiness, stupor or coma).</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:521849:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Bickerstaff&apos;s brainstem encephalitis (BBE) is a rare post-infectious neurological disease characterized by the association of external ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, lower limb arreflexia, extensor plantar response and disturbance of consciousness (drowsiness, stupor or coma).</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:551553:        <obo:IAO_0000115 rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Penetrating and non-penetrating injuries to the spinal cord resulting from traumatic external forces (e.g., WOUNDS, GUNSHOT; WHIPLASH INJURIES; etc.).</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:551615:        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">Penetrating and non-penetrating injuries to the spinal cord resulting from traumatic external forces (e.g., WOUNDS, GUNSHOT; WHIPLASH INJURIES; etc.).</owl:annotatedTarget>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:594873:        <obo:IAO_0000115>the fibromuscular tubular canal through which urine is discharged from the bladder to the exterior via the external urinary meatus; in males, the urethra is joined by the ejaculatory ducts and serves as a passageway for semen during ejaculation, as well as a canal for urine during voiding; in females, the urethra is shorter and emerges above the vaginal opening</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:595275:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The surface (external) layer of ectoderm which begins to proliferate shortly after separation from the neuroectoderm.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:595276:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>surface (external) ectoderm</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:595278:        <rdfs:label>external ectoderm</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:595557:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A glycoprotein membrane surrounding the plasma membrane of an oocyte. It is a vital constitutive part of the latter, external but not extraneous to it. The zona pellucida first appears in multilaminar primary oocytes.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:595635:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A flattened, almost circular bilaminar plate of cells formed when the inner cell mass (aka embryoblast) forms two epithelial layers, each of a distinct lineage, separated by an extracellular basement membrane: the external (dorsal) layer is called the epiblast and the internal (ventral) layer is called the hypoblast (aka primitive endoderm); together, they compose the bilaminar embryonic disc.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:597267:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The external portion of an animal body derived from ectoderm and mesoderm layers that encloses the body cavity.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:597790:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A lining of mostly endodermal origin, covered in epithelium, which is involved in absorption and secretion. They line various body cavities that are exposed to the external environment and internal organs. It is at several places continuous with skin: at the nostrils, the lips, the ears, the genital area, and the anus. The sticky, thick fluid secreted by the mucous membranes and gland is termed mucus. The term mucous membrane refers to where they are found in the body and not every mucous membrane secretes mucus[WP]</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:598083:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The motor and sensory nerve subdivision of the trigeminal nerve that transmits sensory information from the auricle, the external acoustic meatus, tympanic membrane, temporal region, the cheek, the skin overlying the mandible, the anterior portion of the tongue, the floor of the mouth, lower teeth and gingiva and transmits motor information from the muscles of mastication, the mylohyoid muscle and digastric muscle and the muscles tensor tympani and tensor veli palatini.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:599142:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>external secretion</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:601491:        <obo:IAO_0000115>external genital organs of the female mammal[WP].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:602674:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external carotid</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:602676:        <rdfs:label>external carotid artery</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:605389:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The common iliac arteries are two large arteries, about 4cm long in adults but more than a centimetre in diameter, that originate from the aortic bifurcation. The arteries run inferolaterally, along the medial border of the psoas muscles to the pelvic brim, where they bifurcate into the external iliac artery and internal iliac artery. The common iliac artery, and all of its branches, exist as paired structures (that is to say, there is one on the left side and one on the right). The distribution of the common iliac artery is basically the pelvis and lower limb on the corresponding side. Both common iliac arteries are accompanied along their course by common iliac veins. [WP,unvetted].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:607164:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external adventitia of ureter</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:608746:        <obo:IAO_0000115>the external sac of skin that encloses the testes. It is an extension of the abdomen, and in placentals is located between the penis and anus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:608970:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The external iliac artery is a large artery in the pelvic region that carries blood to the lower limb. The external iliac artery is a paired artery, meaning there is one on each side of the body: a right external iliac artery and left external iliac artery. The external iliac artery is accompanied by the external iliac vein, which is located posterior to the artery. [WP,unvetted].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:608972:        <rdfs:label>external iliac artery</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:609085:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A vein that begins near the upper part of the greater sciatic foramen, passes upward behind and slightly medial to the hypogastric artery and, at the brim of the pelvis, joins with the external iliac to form the common iliac vein. [WP,unvetted].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:609744:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external acoustic tube</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:609745:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external auditory canal</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:609746:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external auditory tube</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:609748:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>external auditory meatus</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:609749:        <rdfs:label>external acoustic meatus</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:609775:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The anus and surrounding regions. Encompasses both internal and external regions, where present</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:609906:        <obo:IAO_0000115>In the human body, the femoral vein is a blood vessel that accompanies the femoral artery in the femoral sheath. It begins at the adductor canal (also known as Hunter&apos;s canal) and is a continuation of the popliteal vein. It ends at the inferior margin of the inguinal ligament, where it becomes the external iliac vein. [WP,unvetted].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:610822:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Subdivision of trunk proper, which is demarcated from the neck by the plane of the superior thoracic aperture and from the abdomen internally by the inferior surface of the diaphragm and externally by the costal margin and associated with the thoracic vertebral column and ribcage and from the back of the thorax by the external surface of the posterolateral part of the rib cage, the anterior surface of the thoracic vertebral column and the posterior axillary lines; together with the abdomen and the perineum, it constitutes the trunk proper[FMA].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:611215:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A zone of skin that is part of a external ear [Automatically generated definition].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:611219:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external ear skin</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:611220:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external ear zone of skin</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:611224:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>zone of skin of external ear</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:611226:        <rdfs:label>skin of external ear</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:612959:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A bilaterally paired branched artery that originates from the aortic arches and divides into and includes as parts the internal and external carotid arteries[cjm].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:614227:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The lingual artery arises from the external carotid between the superior thyroid and facial artery[WP].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:614228:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>lingual branch of external carotid artery</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:614303:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A branch of the external carotid artery that supplies structures of the face. [WP,unvetted].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:614305:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>external maxillary artery</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:615518:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The anterior facial vein (facial vein) commences at the side of the root of the nose, and is a direct continuation of the angular vein where it also receives a small nasal branch. It lies behind the facial artery and follows a less tortuous course. It receives blood from the external palatine vein before it either joins the anterior branch of the retromandibular vein to form the common facial vein, or drains directly into the internal jugular vein. [WP,unvetted].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:615557:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The cerebral veins are divisible into external and internal groups according to the outer surfaces or the inner parts of the hemispheres they drain into. The external veins are the superior cerebral veins, inferior cerebral veins, and middle cerebral vein. [WP,unvetted].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:616090:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Part of the ear external to the tympanum (eardrum). It typically consists of a tube (the external auditory meatus) that directs sound waves on to the tympanum, and may also include the external pinna, which extends beyond the skull[GO].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:616095:        <rdfs:label>external ear</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:617627:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The part of the ear that projects from the head, connecting to the external acoustic meatus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:617630:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>auricle of external ear</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:618057:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external epithelium of cornea</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:618612:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external limiting lamina of retina</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:618613:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external limiting membrane</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:618614:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external limiting membrane of retina</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:618617:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>retina external limiting lamina</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:618659:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>external nuclear layer</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:618691:        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>external plexiform layer</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:620388:        <obo:IAO_0000115>cartilage of the pinna and much of the external ear canal.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:620579:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A cartilage that is part of a external ear [Automatically generated definition].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:620580:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external ear cartilage</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:620581:        <rdfs:label>cartilage of external ear</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:621701:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An area of cerebral cortex defined on the basis of cytoarchitecture that have six layers. Starting from the cortical surface the layers are: molecular layer (I), external granular layer (II), external pyramidal layer (III), internal granular layer (IV), internal pyramidal layer (V), and multiform layer (VI). Neocortex is most prominent in the frontal lobe, the parietal lobe, the temporal lobe and the occipital lobe, less so in the cingulate gyrus, the parahippocampal gyrus and the insula. It is composed of two subdivisions: true isocortex and proisocortex (Carpenter-83)(NN)</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:624002:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The femoral artery is a large artery in the muscles of the thigh. It is a continuation of external iliac artery where it enters the femoral triangle at the mid inguinal point behind the inguinal ligament. It leaves femoral triangle through apex beneath the sartorius muscle. It enters the popliteal fossa by passing through the 5th osseo-aponeurotic(adductor hiatus) opening of adductor magnus where it becomes the Popliteal Artery. [WP,unvetted].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:629722:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A thin membrane that separates the external ear from the middle ear, consisting of two epithelia (one part of the the external accoustic meatus epithelium, the other part of the tympanic cavity epithelium) with a fibrous layer between them. Its function is to transmit sound from the air to the ossicles inside the middle ear. The malleus bone bridges the gap between the eardrum and the other ossicles. Rupture or perforation of the eardrum can lead to conductive hearing loss. [WP,unvetted].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:629763:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A gland that secretes products (excluding hormones and other chemical messengers) into ducts (duct glands) which lead directly into the external environment[WP]. Typical exocrine glands include sweat glands, salivary glands, mammary glands, stomach, liver, pancreas</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:630205:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Muscle (organ) which is a part of the abdomen. Examples: external oblique, rectus abdominis.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:631213:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An external caudal extension of the body. In chordates, the tail is post-anal, in other animals the anus ends in the tail</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:631256:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>external covering of organism</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:638679:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>auricle of external ear mesenchyme</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:638684:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>mesenchyme of auricle of external ear</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:648421:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Passages external to the liver for the conveyance of bile. These include the common bile duct and the common hepatic duct.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:652480:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The external masculine genital organs, including the penis and scrotum</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:652481:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external male genital organ</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:652482:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>male external genitalia</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:652484:        <rdfs:label>external male genitalia</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:654018:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The external genitalia are the outer sex organs, such as the penis or vulva in mammals</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:654019:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external genitalia</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:654020:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>external reproductive organ</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:654021:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>external sex organ</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:654022:        <rdfs:label>external genitalia</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:662651:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An epithelium that is part of an external naris [Automatically generated definition].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:662652:        <rdfs:label>external naris epithelium</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:663500:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The glomus forms from the splanchnic intermediate mesoderm and is the vascularized filtration unit, filtering the blood before it enters the tubules. The glomus is external to the nephron and extends over more than one body segment.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:666281:        <obo:IAO_0000115>One of of the pair of folds derived from the cloacal folds which give rise to a portion of the external genitalia; in male embryos they close over the urethral plate and fuse to form the spongy (penile) urethra and ventral aspect of the penis, not including the glans; failure of fusion of the urethral folds leads to hypospadias; in female embryos they fuse only anterior to the anus and form the labia minora[MP].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:667089:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external sphincter muscle of urethra</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:667090:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external sphincter of urethra</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:667091:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external urethral sphincter muscle</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:667096:        <rdfs:label>external urethral sphincter</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:670587:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>tympanic membrane external middle ear cavity epithelial component</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:670962:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An external genitalia that is part of a female reproductive system [Automatically generated definition].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:670963:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external female genital organ</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:670964:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external genitalia of female reproductive system</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:670965:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>female external genitalia</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:670967:        <rdfs:label>external female genitalia</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:671645:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An epithelium is a tissue that covers the internal or external surfaces of an anatomical structure[GO].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:673242:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An external ectoderm that is part of a optic eminence [Automatically generated definition].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:675823:        <obo:IAO_0000115>One of: the common carotid artery, or its branches, the external and internal carotid arteries.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:677317:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A venous channel found between layers of dura mater in the brain. Receives blood from internal and external veins of the brain, receive cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) from the subarachnoid space, and ultimately empty into the internal jugular vein.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:677873:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any of the three veins including the common, external and internal iliac veins.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:679436:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>external coat</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:680549:        <obo:IAO_0000115>the embryonic anlage of the external genitalia, which emerges as paired swellings ventral to the cloacal membrane, is proposed to be derived from all three germ layers, and gives rise to the penis, clitoris, scrotum, labia, and foreskin</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:680582:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An external pocketing of pharyngeal ectoderm that contacts a region of endoderm (a pharyngeal pouch) and interdigitates in the anterior and posterior directions by the pharyngeal arches.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:681009:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A naris that provides one of the small external openings of the head that give passage to air for breathing.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:681011:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external nares</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:681016:        <rdfs:label>external naris</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:683152:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The membrane that covers the embryonic cloaca, formed by the union of proctodeal (anal pit) ectoderm and cloacal endoderm, with no intervening mesoderm; the urorectal septum joins the cloacal membrane and divides it into an anal membrane and a urogenital membrane; the point where the urorectal septum intersects the cloacal membrane is the future site of the perineal body; proliferation of mesoderm and ectoderm around the cloacal membrane produces primordial tissues of the external genitalia in both sexes: the genital tubercle, genital folds, and genital swellings.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:684881:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An external iliac artery branch that supplies blood to the anterior abdominal wall.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:685548:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external orifice of female urethra</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:685549:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external urethral orifice (female)</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:686150:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An external ear that is in the right side of a multicellular organism.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:686152:        <rdfs:label>right external ear</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:686178:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An external ear that is in the left side of a multicellular organism.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:686180:        <rdfs:label>left external ear</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:686252:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The abdominal wall represents the boundaries of the abdominal cavity. The abdominal wall is split into the posterior (back), lateral (sides) and anterior (front) walls. There is a common set of layers covering and forming all the walls: the deepest being the extraperitoneal fat, the parietal peritoneum, and a layer of fascia which has different names over where it covers (eg transversalis, psoas fascia). Superficial to these, but not present in the posterior wall are the three layers of muscle, the transversus abdominis (transverse abdominal muscle), the internal (obliquus internus) and the external oblique (obliquus externus).</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:686519:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Erectile tissue at the end of the clitoris, which is continuous with the intermediate part of the vestibulovaginal bulbs[BTO]. The clitoral glans (glans clitoridis) is an external portion of the clitoris[WP].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:686640:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The galea aponeurotica (epicranial aponeurosis, aponeurosis epicranialis) is a tough layer of dense fibrous tissue which covers the upper part of the cranium; behind, it is attached, in the interval between its union with the Occipitales, to the external occipital protuberance and highest nuchal lines of the occipital bone; in front, it forms a short and narrow prolongation between its union with the Frontales. On either side it gives origin to the Auriculares anterior and superior; in this situation it loses its aponeurotic character, and is continued over the temporal fascia to the zygomatic arch as a layer of laminated areolar tissue. It is closely connected to the integument by the firm, dense, fibro-fatty layer which forms the superficial fascia of the scalp: it is attached to the pericranium by loose cellular tissue, which allows the aponeurosis, carrying with it the integument to move through a considerable distance.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:688505:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Simple columnar epithelium made up of cells specialized to serve as sensory cells for the reception of external stimuli, as the sensory cells of the cochlea, vestibule, nasal mucosa, and tongue.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:690844:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The rhombencephalon (or hindbrain) is a developmental categorization of portions of the central nervous system in vertebrates. The rhombencephalon can be subdivided in a variable number of transversal swellings called rhombomeres. In the human embryo eight rhombomeres can be distinguished, from caudal to rostral: Rh7-Rh1 and the isthmus (the most rostral rhombomere). A rare disease of the rhombencephalon, &apos;rhombencephalosynapsis&apos; is characterized by a missing vermis resulting in a fused cerebellum. Patients generally present with cerebellar ataxia. The caudal rhombencephalon has been generally considered as the initiation site for neural tube closure.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:693814:        <obo:IAO_0000115>External part of the nose containing the external nares.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:693816:        <rdfs:label>external nose</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:694500:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Subdivision of thorax, which in humans is the posterior part of the thorax and is demarcated from the chest by the external surface of the posterolateral part of the rib cage and the anterior surface of the thoracic vertebral column; together with the chest, it constitutes the thorax.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:694745:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The external junctural region between the lower part of the abdomen and the thigh.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:695751:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An external organ of a male organism that is specialized to deliver sperm during copulation. Intromittent organs are found most often in terrestrial species, as most aquatic species fertilize their eggs externally, although there are exceptions[WP].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:695776:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A transient embryonic complex that comprises the pharyngeal arches, bulges of tissues of mesoderm and neural crest derivation through which pass nerves and pharyngeal arch arteries. The arches are separated internally by pharyngeal pouches, evaginations of foregut endoderm, and externally by pharyngeal clefts, invaginations of surface ectoderm. The development of the system ends when the stucture it contributes to are forming, which may include (depending on species) the thymus, thyroid, parathyroids, maxilla, mandible, aortic arch, cardiac outflow tract, external and middle ear[GO,modified].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:696641:        <obo:IAO_0000115>An external projection of an aquatic animal as a fish used in propelling or guiding the body[BTO].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:697753:        <rdfs:label>indifferent external genitalia</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:703446:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A portion of the external acoustic meatus epithelium which makes up the outer (cuticular) layer of the tympanic membrane</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:703451:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>tympanic membrane external acoustic meatus epithelial component</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:704100:        <obo:IAO_0000115>the numerous mucous-secreting glands found in several female mammalian species (including rodents and humans) on the anterior wall of the vagina, around the lower end of the urethra, and possessing a common paraurethral duct which opens (on each side) near the external urethral orifice; they are homologous with the prostate gland in males, have highly variable anatomy, and are believed to be the source of the female ejaculate, a lubricating fluid with a similar consistency to male prostatic fluid that is expelled through the urethra during sexual stimulation; like the male prostate, these glands are susceptible to infection (skenitis), cyst development, and cancer</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:707249:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The integrated unit (of the eye) that consists of the conjunctiva, the corneal surface, and the ocular mucosal adnexa including the lid margins and the meibomian gland openings, the lacrimal glands and the lacrimal drainage system, all which are critical to maintain ocular surface integrity and provide protection from external antigens and pathogenic microorganisms.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:710443:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Am unpaired external naris that lies in the midline of the head.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:710447:        <rdfs:label>median external naris</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:712284:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The mastoid process is a conical prominence projecting from the undersurface of the mastoid portion of the temporal bone. It is located just behind the external acoustic meatus, and lateral to the styloid process. Its size and form vary somewhat; it is larger in the male than in the female. This process serves for the attachment of the posterior belly of the digastric, sternocleidomastoid, splenius capitis, and longissimus capitis muscles. The word is derived from the Greek &apos;masto-&apos;, alluding to its resemblance to the breast.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:713036:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The external sphincter muscle of urethra (or sphincter urethrae membranaceae) surrounds the whole length of the membranous portion of the urethra, and is enclosed in the fasciæ of the urogenital diaphragm. They arch across the front of the urethra and bulbourethral glands, pass around the urethra, and behind it unite with the muscle of the opposite side, by means of a tendinous raphé.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:713037:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external urethral sphincter of male urethra</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:713046:        <rdfs:label>male external urethral sphincter</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:713073:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The external sphincter muscle of female urethra is a muscle which controls urination.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:713074:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external urethral sphincter of female urethra</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:713083:        <rdfs:label>female external urethral sphincter</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:713136:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The pudendal nerve is a somatic nerve in the pelvic region that innervates the external genitalia of both sexes, as well as sphincters for the bladder and the rectum. It originates in Onuf&apos;s nucleus in the sacral region of the spinal cord.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:714032:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Paired structures in the embryo that represent the final stage of development of the caudal end of the external genitals before sexual differentiation. In both males and females the two swellings merge: in the female, they become the posterior labial commissure; In the male, they become the scrotum</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:716986:        <obo:IAO_0000115>external opening or orifice of the urethra through which urine and seminal fluid (in males only) leave the body; in males the meatus presents as a vertical slit normally positioned at the tip of glans penis; in females the meatus is located between the clitoris and the vagina in the vulvular vestibule of the female genitalia[MP].</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:716987:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external meatus of urethra</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:716988:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external urethral orifice</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:716989:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external urethral ostium</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:716990:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external urinary meatus</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:717030:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external orifice of male urethra</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:717031:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external urethral orifice (male)</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:717813:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The median slit located inferior and posterior to the external urethral orifice in the female; the exit for menstrual flow and birth and the entrance for the penis during sexual intercourse; the size and appearance of the vaginal orifice varies inversely with that of the hymen (G. membrane), a thin fold of mucous membrane that surrounds the vaginal orifice</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:718917:        <rdfs:label>external anal region</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:721128:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external to organism</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:721912:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The dartos fascia is a fat-free layer of smooth muscular fiber outside the external spermatic fascia but below the skin. It is a continuation of Scarpa&apos;s Fascia which is a membranous layer of the subcutaneous tissue in the abdominal wall.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:722417:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external os of uterus</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:722419:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>external os</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:722421:        <rdfs:label>external cervical os</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:724365:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The angular vein formed by the junction of the frontal vein and supraorbital vein, runs obliquely downward, on the side of the root of the nose, to the level of the lower margin of the orbit, where it becomes the anterior facial vein. It receives the external nasal veins of the ala nasi, and communicates with the superior ophthalmic vein through the nasofrontal vein, thus establishing an important anastomosis between the anterior facial vein and the cavernous sinus.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:724440:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The deep auricular artery often arises in common with the anterior tympanic artery. It ascends in the substance of the parotid gland, behind the temporomandibular articulation, pierces the cartilaginous or bony wall of the external acoustic meatus, and supplies its cuticular lining and the outer surface of the tympanic membrane. It gives a branch to the temporomandibular joint.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:724883:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external sphincter of esophagus</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:728451:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A zone of skin that is part of a external nose.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:728452:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external nasal skin</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:728453:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>skin of external nose</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:729139:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A epithelium that is part of a external ear.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:729388:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The mastoid lymph nodes (retroauricular lymph nodes, posterior auricular glands), usually two in number, are situated on the mastoid insertion of the Sternocleidomastoideus, beneath the Auricularis posterior. Their afferent vessels drain the posterior part of the temporoparietal region, the upper part of the cranial surface of the auricula or pinna, and the back of the external acoustic meatus; their efferents pass to the superior deep cervical glands.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:729752:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A collagenous capsule covering the external surface of the liver.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:730654:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The external part of the developing pharynx that is made of ectoderm. During vertebrate development, pockets form in pharyngeal ectoderm between the pharyngeal arches.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:734124:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Epithelial layer that is part of the external part of the nose.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:734125:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>epithelium of skin of external nose</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:734126:        <rdfs:label>epithelium of external nose</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:734555:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>hair of external ear</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:734558:        <rdfs:label>strand of hair on external ear</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:735657:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A taste bud that is located external to the digestive tube, on the head or body, as found in species such as goldfish.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:736565:        <rdfs:label>external soft tissue zone</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:737639:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external plantar nerve</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:737922:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Anatomical surface, which is the external surface of the whole body. Examples: There is only one body surface.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:738558:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external carotid arterial subdivision</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:738559:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>subdivision of external carotid artery</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:738560:        <rdfs:label>branch of external carotid artery</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:738850:        <obo:IAO_0000115>The external portion of the prostate including the anterior, inferolateral, lateral and posterior surfaces.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:739486:        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>external acoustic meatus cartilage</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:739487:        <rdfs:label>cartilage of external acoustic meatus</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:742501:        <obo:IAO_0000115>A collection of hairs that grows on the external ear.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:742502:        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>external ear hair</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:742503:        <rdfs:label>collection of hair on external ear</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:742978:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Structures of the dermis, epidermis, glands and pigment cells recognizable on the external surfaces of the integument.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:742979:        <rdfs:label>external integument structure</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:743005:        <obo:IAO_0000115>Dermal, epidermal, glandular and pigment structures of the external head integument.</obo:IAO_0000115>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:743006:        <rdfs:label>head external integument structure</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:743033:        <rdfs:label>body external integument structure</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:743060:        <rdfs:label>limb external integument structure</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:743087:        <rdfs:label>tail external integument structure</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:745007:        <obo:IAO_0000111 xml:lang=\"en\">to be replaced with external ontology term</obo:IAO_0000111>\nsrc/ontology/modules/mondo-harrisons-view.owl:745011:        <rdfs:label xml:lang=\"en\">to be replaced with external ontology term</rdfs:label>\nsrc/ontology/modules/disease_by_location.csv:54:TBD:0000072,external ear disease,UBERON:0001691,external ear\r\nsrc/translations/mondo-jp.babelon.owl:491:        <babelon:source_value>progressive external ophthalmoplegia with mitochondrial DNA deletions</babelon:source_value>\nsrc/translations/mondo-jp.babelon.owl:9331:        <babelon:source_value>external ear lipoma</babelon:source_value>\nsrc/translations/mondo-jp.babelon.owl:24716:        <babelon:source_value>external pathological resorption</babelon:source_value>\nsrc/translations/mondo-jp.babelon.owl:35936:        <babelon:source_value>external ear disorder</babelon:source_value>\nsrc/translations/mondo-jp.babelon.owl:38333:        <