 used when the term is excluded from Mondo based on the fact that the term's concept is not a disease and represents factors and/or actions that influence health status and might (or might not) be the cause of a disease.\tThis code should be used when the concept is not clearly a procedure, an event, or a medical action, or is a mix of these.\tICD10CM:Z00-Z99 - Factors influencing health status and contact with health services\r\nsrc/ontology/config/exclusion_reasons.tsv:11:------ MONDO:excludeCondition\tcondition\tMONDO:excludeExternalFactor\tCode used when the term is excluded from Mondo based on the fact that the term's concept is not a disease and represents a condition possibly leading to disease.\t\tICD10CM:P80-P83 - Conditions involving the integument and temperature regulation of newborn\r\nsrc/ontology/config/exclusion_reasons.tsv:12:------ MONDO:excludeMedicalAction\tmedical action\tMONDO:excludeExternalFactor\tCode used when the term is excluded from Mondo based on the fact that the term's concept is not a disease and represents a clinically prescribed procedure, therapy, intervention, or recommendation.\t\tICD10CM:O30-O48 - Maternal care related to the fetus and amniotic cavity and possible delivery problems ICD10CM:O09-O09 - Supervision of high risk pregnancy\r\nsrc/ontology/config/exclusion_reasons.tsv:13:------ MONDO:excludeEvent\tevent\tMONDO:excludeExternalFactor\tCode used when the term is excluded from Mondo based on the fact that the term's concept is not a disease and represent occurrences; these could result in injury and/or its effect, or represent periods in a disease.\tThis code excludes all procedures and interventions; these should be excluded using the code MONDO:excludeMedicalAction.\tICD10CM:Y62-Y69 - Misadventures to patients during surgical and medical care Orphanet:391655 - Off-periods in Parkinson disease not responding to oral treatment ICD10CM:V98.3 - Accident to, on or involving ski lift\r\nsrc/ontology/config/exclusion_reasons.tsv:29:--------- MONDO:excludeGroupingModifierRare\tgrouping based on rare clinical modifier\tMONDO:excludeGroupingModifier\tCode used when the term is excluded from Mondo based on the fact that this term is not a specific disease; it instead groups diseases based on shared modifier \"rare\". \t- Terms excluded with this code represent grouping terms; actual diseases including the modifier should not be excluded (e.g. Orphanet:102002 - Rare ataxia)  - In some cases, it might be appropriate to include the term as a narrow concept of a Mondo term (e.g. Orphanet:519298-Rare scleral disorder _MONDO:mondoIsBroaderThanSource_ MONDO:0001269-scleral disorder)\tMONDO:0035001 - obsolete rare disorder of the visual organs\r\nsrc/ontology/sources/orphanet/construct-d2g.sparql:21:               owl:onProperty ?ordoRel ;\nsrc/ontology/sources/orphanet/construct-d2g.sparql:32:  VALUES (?ordoRel ?oboRel) {\nsrc/ontology/config/prefixes.csv:228:OMIMPS,https://omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS\nsrc/ontology/config/prefixes.csv:229:OMIM,https://omim.org/entry/\ndocs/editors-guide/sop_splitting-terms.md:18:OMIM may rename a disease from FOO to FOO 1, and create a new phenotypic series (PS) with the name FOO. (For an example, see [OMIM:606176 Diabetes mellitus, permanent neonatal 1](https://www.omim.org/entry/606176), which is part of the phenoypic series [PS606176 Diabetes mellitus, permanent neonatal](https://www.omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS606176) and the respective Mondo ticket [#1803](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/issues/1803). In this case, we should follow the [Splitting a more specific term into a more generic term workflow](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/splitting-classes/#splitting-a-more-specific-term-into-a-more-generic-term).\ndocs/editors-guide/g-logical-axioms.md:5:Use Elk, we generally stay within the EL++ subset. Sometimes UnionOf is used but this is not relied upon in inference.\ndocs/editors-guide/g-logical-axioms.md:19:Rather than removing a subClassOf that comes from a trusted source (like ordo), we usually turn it into an excluded_subClassOf annotation.\ndocs/editors-guide/OMIM-representation.md:28:[OMIM Phenotypic Series](https://www.omim.org/phenotypicSeriesTitles/) (OMIMPS) group diseases that are clinically similar. In Mondo, these OMIMPS entries are represented as “grouping term” (or parent term). Diseases included in OMIMPS are represented in Mondo as subclasses of the Mondo term representing the OMIMPS, with the OMIM ID of disease term as a source evidence for these subclasses. \ndocs/editors-guide/OMIM-representation.md:32:It is important to note that, since these phenotypic series are created by grouping clinically similar diseases, we have occasionally found that, some of these subclasses are not ontologically correct. For example, some OMIMPS include both diseases and susceptibility to diseases (examples: [OMIMPS:162091](https://www.omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS162091), [OMIMPS194070](https://www.omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS194070)). Such “ontologically incorrect” subclasses are manually reviewed and removed (see [“Excluded subclassOf” documentation](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/g-logical-axioms/#excluded-subclassof)). \ndocs/editors-guide/OMIM-representation.md:41:In Mondo, we consider these “OMIM included” entries as separate diseases and therefore represent them by creating specific Mondo entries. Their database cross-reference refers to the main OMIM entries, with the source annotation  “MONDO:includedEntryInOMIM”. (see also OMIM included entries documentation [here](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/omim-included-entries/))\ndocs/editors-guide/OMIM-representation.md:65:The automated process describing the gene-disease association update is [here](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/omim/blob/main/README.md). In short, gene-disease associations are added automatically when a single gene is associated with a disease as reported in the OMIM file (see [here](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/omim/blob/main/README.md) for more details). The reference to the OMIM entry is given as provenance for this annotation.\ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:43:    1. Obsoletion candidate subset: This should always be http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo#obsoletion_candidate\ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:110:1. The owltools command will move all of the dbxrefs to the new term. We can have proxy merges, i.e. two MONDO:equivalentTo xrefs from the same source. If this is the case, one dbxref should be marked as MONDO:preferredExternal (as a source annotation on the dbxref) in addition to the MONDO:equivalentTo source annotation. \ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:125:        1. \"in subset obsoletion candidate\"\ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:127:    1. If there is a rare disease subset annotation from the obsolete term,  it is ok to keep the rare subset annotation to the term kept (we will let the rare disease authorities know so that they can update the source of truth. \ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:155:1. remove all rare disease subset annotations\ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:169:1. Remove all rare disease subset annotations\ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:182:1. Remove all rare disease subset annotations\ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:193:**Note:** The QC test obsolete_warning.sparql will give warnings for classes that do not have subset tags obsoletion_candidate.\ndocs/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting.md:199:There may be rare cases when we chose to reinstate or restore an obsolete class. For example, in this ticket ([#4948](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/issues/4948)) we decided to reinstate some Orphanet grouping classes because they were used in the classification scheme described by the authors of the [paper](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31633310/).\ndocs/editors-guide/synching.md:10:### OMIM sync/slurp\ndocs/editors-guide/synching.md:14:1. We slurp the gene association from OMIM whenever a disease has only 1 gene. We implicitly assume that these cases are monogenic diseases (which may not be fully optimal but will be mostly ok (according to our knowledge of OMIM)). We won't import disease to gene associations if there is more than one gene.\ndocs/editors-guide/synching.md:18:Further instructions for the **OMIM sync/slurp** are documented [here](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/omim-slurp/).\nsrc/ontology/sources/omim/Makefile:3:PRODUCTS = omim.owl\nsrc/ontology/sources/omim/Makefile:4:OMIM_URL=https://github.com/monarch-initiative/omim/releases/latest/download/omim.ttl\nsrc/ontology/sources/omim/Makefile:16:omim-src.ttl:\nsrc/ontology/sources/omim/Makefile:19:omim-src.owl: omim-src.ttl\nsrc/ontology/sources/omim/Makefile:22:omim-src.obo: omim-src.owl\nsrc/ontology/sources/omim/Makefile:23:\trobot convert -i $< --check false -f obo -o $@.tmp.obo && grep -v ^owl-axioms $@.tmp.obo | perl ./bin/fix-omim.pl  > $@\nsrc/ontology/sources/omim/Makefile:27:omim.obo: omim-src.obo\nsrc/ontology/sources/omim/Makefile:28:\t$(OBOGREPL) --neg -r '(equivalent_to|is_a: SO|property_value: RO:0002524)' omim-src.obo | $(OBOGREPL) -r 'property_value: https://w3id.org/biolink/vocab/category https://w3id.org/biolink/vocab/Disease' - | perl -npe 's@https://omim.org/entry/@OMIM:@g;s@https://www.omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS@OMIMPS:@g' |  $(OBOGREPL) -r 'id: OMIM' -   > $@.tmp.obo\nsrc/ontology/sources/omim/Makefile:29:\towltools $@.tmp.obo --set-ontology-id http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/omim.owl -o -f obo $@ && rm $@.tmp.obo\nsrc/ontology/sources/omim/Makefile:30:.PRECIOUS: omim.obo\nsrc/ontology/sources/omim/Makefile:32:omim.owl: omim.obo\nsrc/ontology/sources/omim/Makefile:33:\towltools $< --set-ontology-id http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/omim.owl -o $@\nsrc/ontology/sources/omim/Makefile:35:#omim_sssom.tsv: omim.owl\ndocs/editors-guide/e-design-patterns.md:23:Note there are many cases where Orphanet classifies simultaneously as **both** AD and AR (or inherited and not-inherited). The reasoner will detect this if we mirror these axioms. Some of these are placed in the ordo_inheritance_inconsistent subset. In most cases these were manually resolved by treating the Orphanet class as more generic.\ndocs/editors-guide/e-design-patterns.md:50:* Place speculative into ‘speculative’ subset\ndocs/editors-guide/e-design-patterns.md:59:On examination it was apparent that the AR form was speculative. This may be slated for obsoletion, but for now we placed to speculative subset, classified it using its grandparent terms and added a disease_shares_features_of relationship to (AD) pachyonychia congenita.\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:5:###  qc-animal-disease-rare.sparql\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:17:# Tests if an animal disease made it into the rare disease subset\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:22:    <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo#rare> \ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:23:    <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo#nord_rare>\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:24:    <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo#orphanet_rare>\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:25:    <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo#gard_rare>\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:26:    <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo#doid_rare>\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:27:    <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo#inferred_rare>\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:28:    <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo#mondo_curated_rare>\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:41:    ?entity mondo:excluded_from_qc_check mondoSparqlQcMondo:qc-animal-disease-rare.sparql .\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:106:###  qc-conforms-to-omimps-and-more.sparql\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:154:    FILTER ((str(?source0)=\"MONDO:preferredExternal\"))\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:155:    FILTER ((str(?source4)=\"MONDO:preferredExternal\"))\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:787:###  qc-omim-subsumption.sparql\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:790:prefix omim: <http://identifiers.org/omim/>\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:815:      ?entity mondo:excluded_from_qc_check mondoSparqlQcMondo:qc-omim-subsumption.sparql .\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:830:###  qc-omimps-should-be-inherited.sparql\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:859:     ?entity mondo:excluded_from_qc_check mondoSparqlQcMondo:qc-omimps-should-be-inherited.sparql .\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:870:###  qc-ordo-subset-exact-mapping.sparql\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:878:## This QC check ensures that if we have a source for subset, it must \ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:879:## also be mapped to the same term as the subset\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:882:    VALUES ?subset {\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:883:        <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo#ordo_disorder>\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:885:    ?entity oboInOwl:inSubset ?subset .\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:887:    ?subset_anno a owl:Axiom ;\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:890:           owl:annotatedTarget ?subset ;\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:909:    BIND(str(?subset) as ?property)\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:948:# description: Ensure that preferredExternal annotations are used correctly\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:953:        # Check for cases where there is a preferredExternal annotation\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:958:            oboInOwl:source \"MONDO:preferredExternal\" .\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:966:        # Check for cases where there is a preferredExternal annotation\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:968:        # This indicates that there is no point to saying \"preferredExternal\"\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:973:            oboInOwl:source \"MONDO:preferredExternal\" .\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:1044:###  qc-related-exact-synonym-omim.sparql\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:1853:      \tFILTER ((str(?source11)=\"MONDO:preferredExternal\"))\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:1857:      \tFILTER ((str(?source21)=\"MONDO:preferredExternal\"))\ndocs/editors-guide/quality-control-tests.md:2098:###  qc-undeclared-subset.sparql\nsrc/ontology/sources/medgen/Makefile:34:\towltools $< --extract-subset $* --set-ontology-id $(OBO)/mondo/$@ -o -f obo $@\nsrc/ontology/sources/medgen/bin/medgen2obo.pl:99:    my $ss = mk_subset($_);\nsrc/ontology/sources/medgen/bin/medgen2obo.pl:100:    print \"subsetdef: $ss \\\"$_\\\"\\n\";\nsrc/ontology/sources/medgen/bin/medgen2obo.pl:118:        my $ss = mk_subset($_);\nsrc/ontology/sources/medgen/bin/medgen2obo.pl:119:        print \"subset: $ss\\n\";\nsrc/ontology/sources/medgen/bin/medgen2obo.pl:147:sub mk_subset {\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:3:A rare disease is a disease that affects fewer than 200,000 people in the United States. However, this definition is not universal and varies between rare disease sources, geographical locations, etc. Consequently, rare disease criteria also vary between sources, and one might have difficulties reconciling sources and creating a list for rare diseases. Mondo recognizes these impediments and differences of view. This document explains how Mondo handles rare diseases, how one could get a rare disease list, and the efforts made to bring the community together.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:5:#### What are rare diseases in Mondo?\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:6:There are 3 sources for rare diseases in Mondo:\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:7:1. rare disease authorities\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:11:**1. rare disease authorities**\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:12:We rely on rare disease authorities (see table below) to determine diseases recognized as “rare”. These rare diseases are determined by the rare disease authorities according to their own criteria. When a disease is considered “rare” according to a source, the corresponding Mondo term is annotated with an `in_subset` annotation expressing that this disease is considered rare according to this source: `X_rare`  (where X is the rare disease authority). These rare disease subset annotations are maintained automatically via rare disease lists from the authorities.  \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:14:Example: 'calciphylaxis' (MONDO:0017215) is considered \"rare\" according to NORD, GARD, and Orphanet, as shown by the 'in_subset' annotations 'nord_rare', 'gard_rare', 'orphanet_rare'.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:15:![rare230804](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/assets/12737987/e8d6f16c-0d58-4867-bafb-2755def10b8b)\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:19:In some occasional cases, some diseases are reported in the literature as 'rare', though they are not reported in the rare disease authorities' list. In these cases, we record the disease as rare by manually adding the \"mondo_curated_rare\" subset annotation to the term. These manually curated annotations must be supported by the literature. This new rare disease is reported to the rare disease authorities. \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:22:Ontological parents pass their characteristics (here: axioms) to their children. We leverage this ontological property for rare diseases: ontological children of a term that is considered \"rare\" according to the rules above are getting the \"inferred_rare\" subset annotation.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:25:#### The Mondo rare disease subset includes ALL rare diseases according to ALL rare disease sources.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:26:The Mondo rare disease subset represents the UNION of rare diseases according to all authority sources, manual curation, and ontology inference. It should be noted that most of the diseases in this subset will be viewed as “rare” by _most_ authorities, but some will be viewed as “rare” by _only some_ authorities. \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:28:Rare disease terms in Mondo are annotated with `in_subset: rare` (see arrowhead in image above), and include\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:30:- terms with the annotation in_subset ‘X_rare’ (which includes ALL the rare disease authorities represented in Mondo)\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:31:- terms with the annotation in_subset ‘mondo_curated_rare’ (representing the manually curated rare diseases)\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:32:- terms with the annotation in_subset 'inferred_rare' (representing the diseases  ontologically inferred as rare based on their parents)\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:34:Users should decide which subset they want to use for their applications. They can do so by filtering the rare disease subset by any of the 'in_subset' annotations. \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:36:#### Where to find the Mondo rare disease subset, and what is included in the file?\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:37:The Mondo rare disease can be found as a product of the Mondo release, available in .obo and .json formats\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:38:- [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/subsets/mondo-rare.obo](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/subsets/mondo-rare.obo)\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:39:- [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/subsets/mondo-rare.json](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/subsets/mondo-rare.json)\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:41:It should be noted that these rare disease subset files include, in addition to the \"rare diseases\" (as defined above), the ontological parents (ie subclasses) of these rare diseases. These ontological parents allow to view the rare disease subset in a higher classification. \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:43:To get **only** the rare diseases, one should filter the subset files for terms annotated with \"subset:rare\" (or the subset annotation refering to the rare disease authoritative source of choice).\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:45:#### What happens when a rare disease term is obsoleted or loses its \"rare\" characteristic?\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:46:Terms in Mondo can be obsoleted for multiple reasons (see obsoletion documents [here](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting/)). It is possible that some of these terms to be obsoleted are in the rare disease subset. \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:48:1. If a MONDO term (MONDO id) is obsoleted, the Mondo term will not appear in the rare disease subset, regardless of whether the disease is considered rare by one of the authoritative rare disease sources. The rare disease authoritative source reporting this disease will be contacted to report this obsoletion and its reasons.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:49:2. If an authoritative rare source obsoletes a disease term or removes a disease from their rare disease list, the corresponding Mondo term will lose its corresponding \"rare subset\" annotation for this particular source. It should be noted that the rare subset annotation for the other authorities will not be modified unless they also obsolete or remove the corresponding disease from their rare disease list.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:50:3. If a MONDO term (Mondo-1) is merged with another term (Mondo-2), its rare subset annotations will be transferred to this term (Mondo-2)\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:52:#### Current rare disease authority sources represented in Mondo\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:54:| Rare disease authority | link | in_subset annotation in Mondo | Rare disease source criteria |\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:56:| GARD | [https://rarediseases.info.nih.gov/](https://rarediseases.info.nih.gov/) | gard_rare | *(coming soon)*|\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:57:| NORD | [https://rarediseases.org/](https://rarediseases.org/) | nord_rare | *(coming soon)*|\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset.md:58:| Orphanet | [https://www.orpha.net/](https://www.orpha.net/) | orphanet_rare | *(coming soon)*|\ndocs/editors-guide/exclusion-reasons.md:33:| --- MONDO:excludeExternalFactor             | external factor                                          | Code used when the term is excluded from Mondo based on the fact that the term's concept is not a disease and represents factors and/or actions that influence health status and might (or might not) be the cause of a disease.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                | This code should be used when the concept is not clearly a procedure, an event, or a medical action, or is a mix of these.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  | ICD10CM:Z00-Z99\t- Factors influencing health status and contact with health services                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |                                     TRUE                                    |\ndocs/editors-guide/exclusion-reasons.md:53:| --------- MONDO:excludeGroupingModifierRare | grouping based on rare clinical modifier                 | Code used when the term is excluded from Mondo based on the fact that this term is not a specific disease; it instead groups diseases based on shared modifier \"rare\".                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | - Terms excluded with this code represent grouping terms; actual diseases including the modifier should not be excluded (e.g. Orphanet:102002 - Rare ataxia)  - In some cases, it might be appropriate to include the term as a narrow concept of a Mondo term (e.g. Orphanet:519298-Rare scleral disorder _MONDO:mondoIsBroaderThanSource_ MONDO:0001269-scleral disorder)                                                                                                                                                 | MONDO:0035001 - obsolete rare disorder of the visual organs                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |                                    FALSE                                    |\ndocs/editors-guide/splitting-classes.md:13:1) A grouping class or phenotypic series (PS) is created to group more specific classes and the original class becomes ambiguous. (This usually applies to OMIM). For example, OMIM may rename a disease from FOO to FOO 1, and create a new phenotypic series (PS) with the name FOO. (For an example, see [OMIM:606176 Diabetes mellitus, permanent neonatal 1](https://www.omim.org/entry/606176), which is part of the PS [PS606176 Diabetes mellitus, permanent neonatal](https://www.omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS606176) and the respective [Mondo ticket #1803](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/issues/1803). In this case, we should follow the  **_Splitting a more specific term into a more generic term_** workflow.\ndocs/editors-guide/splitting-classes.md:35:There is a specific use case where OMIM creates a phenotypic series (PS); \"a Phenotypic Series is a tabular view of genetic heterogeneity of similar phenotypes across the genome. The link is available under the Phenotype-Gene mini-table in many phenotype entries. A list of disorders with a phenotypic series is available [here](https://www.omim.org/phenotypicSeriesTitles/all).\" ([OMIM FAQs](https://www.omim.org/help/faq#1_13)).\ndocs/editors-guide/splitting-classes.md:41:1. If you have a term in Mondo, such as FOO1, and there is now a more general grouping class, [obsolete](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/merging-and-obsoleting/#obsolete-a-class-without-merging) the old term and create two new terms; one for the parent class and the other for the child class (the more specific class). _For example: [OMIM:606176 Diabetes mellitus, permanent neonatal 1](https://www.omim.org/entry/606176) is now part of the PS [PS606176 Diabetes mellitus, permanent neonatal](https://www.omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS606176)._\ndocs/editors-guide/splitting-classes.md:48:1. If you have a term in Mondo, such as FOO, and there are now more specific subtypes, such as FOO1, FOO2 (which could be new diseases with specific gene mutations), create the new class for the child(ren) term(s). _For example, MONDO:0009853 Imerslund-Grasbeck syndrome, which is equivalent to [OMIMPS:PS261100](https://www.omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS261100). The children terms are MONDO:0009853 Imerslund-Grasbeck syndrome 1 (which is equivalent to [OMIM:261100](https://www.omim.org/entry/261100) and MONDO:0100127 Imerslund-Grasbeck syndrome 2 (which is equivalent to [OMIM:618882](https://www.omim.org/entry/618882)._\ndocs/editors-guide/update-external-sources.md:7:1. run ingest: https://ci.monarchinitiative.org/job/build-omim/ note: right now only a few people can actually run this and it is likely to be replaced by the new ingest being developed by Dazhi soon. For the moment, ping Nico to run this ingest. Before continuing to step 2, make sure that the run has completed by checking that the last run in the [build history](https://ci.monarchinitiative.org/job/build-omim/) is green. \ndocs/editors-guide/update-external-sources.md:9:3. In terminal: `sh run.sh make omim_slurp -B`\ndocs/editors-guide/update-external-sources.md:10:4. If successful this command will create two files: mondo/src/ontology/tmp/ps_slurp_omim.obo and mondo/src/ontology/tmp/rest_slurp_omim.obo\ndocs/editors-guide/update-external-sources.md:11:5. Add all the classes from the slurped files to mondo-edit.obo. (Use a text editor, rathe than Protege). \ndocs/editors-guide/update-external-sources.md:12:6. If ps_slurp_omim.obo is not empty, go back to step 3 and run the slurp again until the ps_slurp_omim.obo is empty\ndocs/editors-guide/update-external-sources.md:19:1. In terminal: `sh run.sh make ordo_slurp -B`\ndocs/editors-guide/update-external-sources.md:20:1. If successful this command will create two files: mondo/src/ontology/tmp/ps_slurp_ordo.obo and mondo/src/ontology/tmp/rest_slurp_ordo.obo. Note: ps_slurp_ordo.obo should always be empty\ndocs/editors-guide/update-external-sources.md:21:1. Add all the classes from the slurped files to mondo-edit.obo \ndocs/editors-guide/update-external-sources.md:31:1. Terms in the OMIM slurp include RELATED synonyms in the format TERM IN ALL CAPS; ABBREVIATION, for example: `synonym: \"MEGACYSTIS-MICROCOLON-INTESTINAL HYPOPERISTALSIS SYNDROME 2; MMIHS2\" RELATED  [OMIM:619351]`. \ndocs/editors-guide/add-new-terms.md:125:### Add subset tags\ndocs/editors-guide/add-new-terms.md:127:1. See a description of subsets [here](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/f-entities/#subsets).\ndocs/editors-guide/add-new-terms.md:128:1. To add a subset tag, click the + above Annotations in the Annotations box\ndocs/editors-guide/add-new-terms.md:129:1. Chose in_subset in the annotations list \ndocs/editors-guide/add-new-terms.md:132:1. Scroll down to subset_property and expand the list\ndocs/editors-guide/add-new-terms.md:133:1. Chose subset tag, such as obsoletion_candidate\ndocs/editors-guide/related-documentation.md:18:| [OMIM Ingest Documentation - General Build](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Dwhh4ZU6znoh-4BUfJrL_t4084ElKcUfLq3UIehNtMk/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.jmd7l1nkdnbi) | Describes the process to ingest data from OMIM and create data release files within the [monarch-initiative/omim repo](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/omim) | Mondo developers |\ndocs/editors-guide/related-documentation.md:20:| [Externally Managed Content](https://docs.google.com/document/d/14iPK1LNgXbrzD92n6S7MKsGs2DKnuURRqapyfQfdx0E/edit?tab=t.0) | Describes how to run the Externally Managed Content(EMC) pipeline in order to ingest content from trusted sources into Mondo. | Mondo developers |\ndocs/editors-guide/import-terms-for-logical-axioms.md:46:- Check if the new NCBI Taxon identifier(s) also exist in the [taxon-subset-ids.txt](https://github.com/obophenotype/ncbitaxon/blob/master/subsets/taxon-subset-ids.txt) file in the \"obophenotype/ncbitaxon/subsets\" repo\ndocs/editors-guide/import-terms-for-logical-axioms.md:47:    - If the identifiers are not in the file, update the `taxon-subset-ids.txt` file to add the identifiers and create a PR in the \"obophenotype/ncbitaxon/subsets\" repo to include the new identifiers  \ndocs/editors-guide/import-terms-for-logical-axioms.md:49:    - This additional step is needed since we are not using NCBI Taxon directly, but the OBO slim, and the [taxon-subset-ids.txt](https://github.com/obophenotype/ncbitaxon/blob/master/subsets/taxon-subset-ids.txt) file is the seed of the NCBITaxon slim.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:3:Rare diseases in Mondo are created based on 3 main subsets. Rules for editing these subsets depends on the subset type. Before editing rare disease information in Mondo, please familiarize yourself with how rare diseases are represented in Mondo [here](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset/).\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:5:It is important to remember that the rare disease subsets are updated automatically at the time of the monthly Mondo release. It is, therefore, crucial that editing rare disease information is done by following the instructions below for these edits to be retained after the release.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:8:These terms have an `in_subset` annotation `X_rare`  (where X is the rare disease authority).\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:9:These subsets are maintained automatically:\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:10:- a list of rare diseases is maintained for each source here:\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:11:    - src/ontology/subsets/nord-subset.template.tsv\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:12:    - src/ontology/subsets/gard-subset.template.tsv\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:13:- during the Mondo release, the `X_rare` subset is updated based on the Mondo terms on the authoritative source list\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:15:Until we have set a process with the sources to maintain this list automatically, Mondo editors should update this list of rare diseases manually **upon request by the authoritative source**.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:16:1. Create or open a GitHub issue reported by the authoritative source (ie NORD, GARD) to add or remove a term from the authoritative rare disease list.   \ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:17:1. Open the rare disease list (from src/ontology/subsets) in a text editor:\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:18:     1. To add a new term, add a new line with the Mondo ID to add to the rare subset. Please make sure that the line is similar to the previous one: this document is a ROBOT template, and therefore the information in each column is important.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:20:1. The term will be added to the rare disease list once the PR is merged, but the subset annotation will not be added until the monthly Mondo release has run.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:22:Note: rare diseases from Orphanet are maintained by a completely automated process. If an update is needed, please create a GitHub issue for the technical team.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:24:**2. Manually curated rare diseases.**\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:25:These terms have an `in_subset` annotation `mondo_curated_rare`, and should be added manually when they are reported in the literature as 'rare', but they are not reported in the rare disease authorities' list.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:28:   - Annotation property: in_subset\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:29:   - Entity IRI: mondo_curated_rare (note that this term is “subset_property” annotation property branch)\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:31:2. Add sources for this annotation (it is important to document how a term was determined as rare). Sources MUST include\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:34:3. Share the GH issue reporting this term as a rare disease with the rare disease authoritative sources (by bringing their attention to this term, they might want to add it to their rare disease list).\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:36:**3. Inferred rare disease.**\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:37:These terms have an `in_subset` annotation `inferred_rare`. This subset is created automatically and refers to the ontological children of rare disease terms.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:38:This subset should **NOT** be manually curated.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:41:#### Note about rare disease subset during term obsoletion/merging.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:42:Specific rules exist surrounding obsoletion and merging of terms that are in the rare disease subset (see [here](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/rare-disease-subset/) and below).\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:43:Since rare disease subsets (except for the mondo_curated_rare subset) are maintained automatically or semi-automatically, obsoleting/merging terms from the rare disease subset should be done with extreme caution.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:44:When obsoleting a term that has \"rare\" subset annotations, the \"rare\" subset annotations should be maintained on the obsoleted term.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:47:**Obsoletion/merging of rare disease terms: rules.**\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:48:1. If a MONDO term (MONDO id) is obsoleted, the Mondo term will not appear in the rare disease subset, regardless of whether the disease is considered rare by one of the authoritative rare disease sources.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:49:2. If an authoritative rare source obsoletes a disease term or removes a disease from their rare disease list, the corresponding Mondo term will lose its corresponding \"X_rare\" subset annotation for this particular source.\ndocs/editors-guide/rare-disease-curator.md:50:3. If a MONDO term (Mondo-1) is merged with another term (Mondo-2), its rare subset annotations will be transferred to this term (Mondo-2).\ndocs/editors-guide/pre-release-checklist.md:3:1. Run the [OMIM slurp](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/migration-workflow/) workflow.\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/rare_genetic.md:1:# rare_genetic \ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/rare_genetic.md:3:[http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/patterns/rare_genetic.yaml](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/patterns/rare_genetic.yaml)\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/rare_genetic.md:11:rare genetic {[disease](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000001)}\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/rare_genetic.md:15:* [exact_synonym](http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym): rare inborn {[disease](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000001)}\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/rare_genetic.md:17:* [exact_synonym](http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym): rare constitutional {[disease](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000001)}\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/rare_genetic.md:21:A form of {[disease](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000001)} that is both rare and genetic.\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/rare_genetic.md:25:{[disease](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000001)} and [has characteristic](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053) some [genetic](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0021150) and [has characteristic](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053) some [rare](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0021136)\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/rare.md:1:# rare \ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/rare.md:3:[http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/patterns/rare.yaml](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/patterns/rare.yaml)\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/rare.md:11:rare {[disease](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000001)}\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/rare.md:15:* [exact_synonym](http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym): rare {[disease](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000001)}\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/rare.md:19:Any of the forms of {[disease](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000001)} that have a rare incidence.\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/rare.md:23:{[disease](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0000001)} and [has characteristic](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053) some [rare](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0021136)\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/inborn_metabolic.md:16:* [related_synonym](http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym): rare inborn error of {[process](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000015)}\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/inborn_metabolic_disrupts.md:20:* [related_synonym](http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym): rare inborn error of {[process](http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing)}\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/disease_series_by_gene_and_inheritance.md:8:Examples: [Growth hormone insensitivity syndrome with immune dysregulation](https://omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS245590), Growth hormone insensitivity with immune dysregulation 1, autosomal recessive and Growth hormone insensitivity with immune dysregulation 2, autosomal dominant\nsrc/ontology/subsets/mondo-otar-subset.template.tsv:1:mondo_id\tsubset\tsubset_source\timpact\r\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/index.md:38:| [disease_series_by_gene_and_inheritance](disease_series_by_gene_and_inheritance/) | This pattern is for diseases that are caused by a single mutation in a single gene, that have gene-based names, and are inherited by a specific mechanism, succh as autosomal dominant and autosomal recessive. <br/>Examples: [Growth hormone insensitivity syndrome with immune dysregulation](https://omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS245590), Growth hormone insensitivity with immune dysregulation 1, autosomal recessive and Growth hormone insensitivity with immune dysregulation 2, autosomal dominant | \ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/index.md:81:| [OMIM_phenotypic_series](OMIM_phenotypic_series/) | This pattern is meant to be used for OMIM phenotypic series (OMIMPS), which are represented as grouping classes in Mondo. Note:  - every instance of this metaclass should be equivalent to (via annotated xref) to something in OMIMPS namespace - it will never have an asserted causative gene as logical axiom (and no single causative gene in text def) - it must never be equivalent to an OMIM:nnnnnn (often redundant with the above rule) - it must have an acronym synonym, e.g. HPE - it must have two or more subclasses (direct or indirect) that are equivalent to OMIMs - the subclasses should (not must) have a logical def that uses the PS as a genus (see [disease_series_by_gene.yaml](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/patterns/disease_series_by_gene.yaml)) - the OMIM subclasses must have acronym synonyms that are the parent syn + number, e.g. HPE1, HPE2 - the primary label for the children should also be parent + {\"type\"} + number - the first member will usually have the same number local ID as the PS - the first member in OMIM usually has documentation that is pertinent to the parent PS - the members may(?) generally share high semantic similarity - All OMIMPS disease should have a has characteristic some inherited restricted, see [omimps-should-be-inherited-violation.sparql](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/sparql/omimps-should-be-inherited-violation.sparql)<br/>Examples: [holoprosencephaly](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0016296) [OMIMPS:236100](https://omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS236100), '3-M syndrome'(http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0007477) [OMIMPS:236100](https://omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS273750).  | \ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/index.md:90:| [rare](rare/) | TBD. | \ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/index.md:91:| [rare_genetic](rare_genetic/) | TBD. | \ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/OMIM_phenotypic_series.md:6:This pattern is meant to be used for OMIM phenotypic series (OMIMPS), which are represented as grouping classes in Mondo. Note:  - every instance of this metaclass should be equivalent to (via annotated xref) to something in OMIMPS namespace - it will never have an asserted causative gene as logical axiom (and no single causative gene in text def) - it must never be equivalent to an OMIM:nnnnnn (often redundant with the above rule) - it must have an acronym synonym, e.g. HPE - it must have two or more subclasses (direct or indirect) that are equivalent to OMIMs - the subclasses should (not must) have a logical def that uses the PS as a genus (see http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/patterns/disease_series_by_gene.yaml) - the OMIM subclasses must have acronym synonyms that are the parent syn + number, e.g. HPE1, HPE2 - the primary label for the children should also be parent + {\"type\"} + number - the first member will usually have the same number local ID as the PS - the first member in OMIM usually has documentation that is pertinent to the parent PS - the members may(?) generally share high semantic similarity - All OMIMPS disease should have a has characteristic some inherited restricted, see http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo/sparql/omimps-should-be-inherited-violation.sparql\ndocs/editors-guide/patterns/OMIM_phenotypic_series.md:8:Examples: [holoprosencephaly](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0016296) [OMIMPS:236100](https://omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS236100), '3-M syndrome'(http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0007477) [OMIMPS:236100](https://omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS273750). \ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:1:## Automatic Mondo Quality Control Checks for Externally Managed Content\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:2:A subset of the extensive Mondo QC checks are automatically run on the externally managed content (EMC). To limit delay due to QC failure, any content from external providers that does not pass these QC checks is not included in Mondo until the content is updated by the data providers and passes the QC checks. Reports of these QC checks will be available for review of any errors so that any needed changes can be made.\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:4:### QC Checks for Externally Managed Content\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:12:| qc-animal-disease-rare| An animal disease is contained within a rare disease subset. |<a href='https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/blob/master/src/sparql/qc/mondo/qc-animal-disease-rare.sparql' target='_blank'>animal disease rare</a>|\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:19:# QC Failure Report for nord.robot.tsv\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:23:| mondo_id      | report_ref   | report_ref_source   | preferred_name             | preferred_name_source   | synonym_type   | subset   | subset_source   | subset_source2   | Source         | Check                                               |\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:25:| MONDO:0001280 |              |                     | Posterior Uveitis          |                         |                |          |                 |                  | nord.robot.tsv | duplicate_scoped_synonym (oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym) |\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:26:| MONDO:0002119 |              |                     | Juvenile Ossifying Fibroma |                         |                |          |                 |                  | nord.robot.tsv | duplicate_scoped_synonym (oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym) |\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:27:| MONDO:0005731 |              |                     | Acanthocheilonemiasis      |                         |                |          |                 |                  | nord.robot.tsv | duplicate_scoped_synonym (oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym) |\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:28:| MONDO:0005761 |              |                     | Elephantiasis              |                         |                |          |                 |                  | nord.robot.tsv | duplicate_scoped_synonym (oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym) |\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:29:| MONDO:0007030 |              |                     | Aarskog Syndrome           |                         |                |          |                 |                  | nord.robot.tsv | duplicate_scoped_synonym (oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym) |\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:30:| MONDO:0007306 |              |                     | Klippel-Feil Syndrome      |                         |                |          |                 |                  | nord.robot.tsv | duplicate_scoped_synonym (oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym) |\ndocs/editors-guide/externally-managed-content.md:31:| MONDO:0007389 |              |                     | Spondylothoracic Dysplasia |                         |                |          |                 |                  | nord.robot.tsv | duplicate_scoped_synonym (oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym) |\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:73:These are incorrect in many places where they have been brought in externally. **Do not trust scope if there is no synonym xref other than DO**.\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:195:MONDO:includedEntryInOMIM | This indicates a term is an 'included' entry in OMIM, for example https://omim.org/entry/233910 | xrefs | N | MONDO:0100167 'dystonia, dopa-responsive, with or without hyperphenylalaninemia, autosomal recessive'\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:210:MONDO:preferredExternal | Identifies the preferred term in case multiple terms from the same source are merged (aka proxy merge). | database_cross_reference | Y | MONDO:0009666 'holocarboxylase synthetase deficiency' database_cross_reference: SCTID:360369003\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:217:We use ORDO subClassOf as subset, thus we have subsets starting ordo_ for all their disease metaclassifications: group_of_disorders, etiological_subtype etc. We retain provenance as an axiom annotation.\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:219:We add our own subsets. These should be described in the header. They include subsets of classes that need further examination.\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:221:We use the n_of_one subset if a disease is described only in one individual or family. We try and retain these unless it is something truly lost in the midsts of time.\ndocs/editors-guide/f-entities.md:229:Susceptibility to diseases should be classified under MONDO:0042489 'disease susceptibility' (or MONDO:0020573 'inherited disease susceptibility', as appropriate). We should group any OMIM susceptibility terms under a susceptibility to disease term. We will not add OMIM xrefs to these, even if the term is part of the OMIMPS. For example, https://www.omim.org/phenotypicSeries/PS161400 - we will create a new class called narcolepsy, susceptibility but will NOT refer back to the OMIMPS. (See related ticket: [https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/issues/5511](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo/issues/5511)).\ndocs/editors-guide/migration-workflow.md:3:1. In mondo-ingest GitHub, navigate to src/ontology/slurp\ndocs/editors-guide/migration-workflow.md:4:1. Open slurp tsv file in google docs, such as [https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo-ingest/blob/main/src/ontology/slurp/ordo.tsv](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo-ingest/blob/main/src/ontology/slurp/ordo.tsv).\ndocs/editors-guide/migration-workflow.md:24:   1. Review all tables named `unmapped_%_lex` (for example, `unmapped_omim_lex`) and `unmapped_%_lex_exact` (for example, `unmapped_omim_lex_exact`)\ndocs/editors-guide/migration-workflow.md:27:   1. go to link: [https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo-ingest/tree/main/src/ontology/slurp](https://github.com/monarch-initiative/mondo-ingest/tree/main/src/ontology/slurp)\ndocs/editors-guide/bulk-removal.md:53:| id | label | Obsoletion candidate subset | obsoletion date\ndocs/editors-guide/bulk-removal.md:78:|MONDO:0002336\t|obsolete inflammatory and toxic neuropathy\t|out of scope\t|MONDO:excludeConditionsCausedByExternalForce\ndocs/editors-guide/omim-included-entries.md:5:OMIM records have 'included' entries that are described as 'Other entities represented in this entry' on the top of the page, for example [https://omim.org/entry/233910](https://omim.org/entry/233910). These represent distinct, but often related diseases, and information about the disease is included with the OMIM entry and it is not someplace else. An 'included' label in OMIM is not as an alternate title. In most cases, the same genes are implicated for the proper disease and the 'included' term.\ndocs/editors-guide/cite.md:3:Nicole A Vasilevsky, Nicolas A Matentzoglu, Sabrina Toro, Joseph E Flack IV, Harshad Hegde, Deepak R Unni, Gioconda F Alyea, Joanna S Amberger, Larry Babb, James P Balhoff, Taylor I Bingaman, Gully A Burns, Orion J Buske, Tiffany J Callahan, Leigh C Carmody, Paula Carrio Cordo, Lauren E Chan, George S Chang, Sean L Christiaens, Louise C Daugherty, Michel Dumontier, Laura E Failla, May J Flowers, H. Alpha Garrett Jr., Jennifer L Goldstein, Dylan Gration, Tudor Groza, Marc Hanauer, Nomi L Harris, Jason A Hilton, Daniel S Himmelstein, Charles Tapley Hoyt, Megan S Kane, Sebastian Köhler, David Lagorce, Abbe Lai, Martin Larralde, Antonia Lock, Irene López Santiago, Donna R Maglott, Adriana J Malheiro, Birgit H M Meldal, Monica C Munoz-Torres, Tristan H Nelson, Frank W Nicholas, David Ochoa, Daniel P Olson, Tudor I Oprea, David Osumi-Sutherland, Helen Parkinson, Zoë May Pendlington, Ana Rath, Heidi L Rehm, Lyubov Remennik, Erin R Riggs, Paola Roncaglia, Justyne E Ross, Marion F Shadbolt, Kent A Shefchek, Morgan N Similuk, Nicholas Sioutos, Damian Smedley, Rachel Sparks, Ray Stefancsik, Ralf Stephan, Andrea L Storm, Doron Stupp, Gregory S Stupp, Jagadish Chandrabose Sundaramurthi, Imke Tammen, Darin Tay, Courtney L Thaxton, Eloise Valasek, Jordi Valls-Margarit, Alex H Wagner, Danielle Welter, Patricia L Whetzel, Lori L Whiteman, Valerie Wood, Colleen H Xu, Andreas Zankl, Xingmin Aaron Zhang, Christopher G Chute, Peter N Robinson, Christopher J Mungall, Ada Hamosh, Melissa A Haendel. **Mondo: Unifying diseases for the world, by the world**, medRxiv 2022.04.13.22273750; doi: https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.04.13.22273750\ndocs/editors-guide/strategic-refinement.md:11:1. [Review ORDO grouping classes for obsoletion](https://mondo.readthedocs.io/en/latest/editors-guide/strategic-refinement/#review-ordo-grouping-classes-for-obsoletion)\ndocs/editors-guide/regex.md:99:**Description:** By default, OMIM terms and synonyms are written as the long name and abbreviation combined, for example [Barber-Say syndrome, BBRSAY](https://omim.org/entry/209885). This will split the synonym and abbreviation, for example Barber-Say syndrome and BBRSAY (see [MONDO:0008853](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MONDO_0008853)). This should be followed by adding the abbreviation tag to the abbreviation synonyms (see above).\nsrc/ontology/subsets/gard-subset.template.tsv:1:mondo_id\ttype\tgard_id\tsource_external\tsubset\tsubset_source\nsrc/ontology/subsets/gard-subset.template.tsv:3:MONDO:0000023\towl:Class\tGARD:17820\tOrphanet:464724\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo#gard_rare\tGARD:17820\nsrc/ontology/subsets/gard-subset.template.tsv:4:MONDO:0000044\towl:Class\tGARD:6735\tOrphanet:437\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo#gard_rare\tGARD:6735\nsrc/ontology/subsets/gard-subset.template.tsv:5:MONDO:0000050\towl:Class\tGARD:12556\tOrphanet:631\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo#gard_rare\tGARD:12556\nsrc/ontology/subsets/gard-subset.template.tsv:6:MONDO:0000087\towl:Class\tGARD:18818\tOrphanet:35981\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/mondo#gard_rare\tGARD:18818\nsrc/ontology/subsets/gard-subset.template.tsv:7:MONDO:0000088\towl:Class\tGARD:7446\tOrphanet:9